- *The tracked passage (old 12:137–153) is re-entered, not reworded.* New order: Karis checks her copy and lays the sheet back → the lamp smokes, Brom says so, Lira pinches the wick → Cael looks for Seln and does not find him where he was → Karis asks him a practical question about the date's form → "I have read it," (12:149), before a word about stillness → Cael goes back over it: the others were looking at the bar; Seln's eyes were on her pencil, and he stopped when it came to the figures → "It was the date, then, and not the stripe." → the scene ends on a thing declined: "He stayed where he was. There was one paper in that room which he had not let himself touch all evening, and he did not go looking for a second." Gone: the definition of his usual stillness, the look, and the list of other unanswered things. Required facts kept: he went still at the date; he does not say the sum; nobody connects it to anyone; Cael does not know what it was.
- Consequences elsewhere: Karis no longer compares the sheet with her copy in the last scene (the sheet is back in its row); she writes under her copy "where the sheet itself now lay, and who had laid it there" (12:271). The inventory of the table reads "a courier's leather with six rows in it, and a barred sheet at the foot of the second, and beside the leather a broker's paper" (12:231).

**B10. Custody (12:173, 12:299).** Receipt, **before:** "…the sheets not yet counted, and one broker's report; one leaf cut out of a register; one technician's sheet, copied this night. Examined by nobody. — K." **After:** "*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*" Log, **before:** "…six years of an office's counting… one line, cut out of a register… Karis has all three and has read none of them." **After:** "…six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet." Karis's last speech: "an office's count that I cannot read… a box with…" → "six years of an office's sheets under that same index, and a title that I have never met, and a box that I have ruled round nothing" (12:295).

**B11. The broker's report (11:15; 12:11).** 11:15, **before:** "It had this town's name on it, and five others." **After:** "It had this town's name on it, and a board's; and where a crew's name should have stood, it had five strokes, and a count of six… I knew of only one five who had gone that way." 12:11, **before:** "…a town on no registry map, and a board, and five names." **After:** "…a town on no registry map, and a board; and where a crew's name should have been there were five strokes, and after the strokes a count, which was six." "That's us… In a folder." stands. Consistent with 10:55, where he has only two of their names, and those from a file.

**B12. The plate on the bluff (8:181).** **Before:** "He had once had a boy stood on one, on a bluff, and watched it stutter and go dark; and when the form came round that would have stood the boy there a second time, he had declined." **After:** "He had once carried a plate up a bluff, with a file and a set of questions, to a boy, and all three had told him nothing; and when the form came round that would have had the boy read a second time, he had declined." This is Book 5 ch58:229 and no more. 11:91 already says only that he "sat four feet from him for three days, asking", and that he declined when a form "would have stood Cael on a plate a second time"; I left it.

**B13. Chapter 7's opening.** Added at 7:7, before the first spoken word: "He had his back to the far bank, and he was aware of it the whole time, as a man is aware of a door behind him. Three nights ago a small pale woman had asked him, at a board, to go back over that river, to a town inside the Line with a gate and a book in it. He had said that he would rule, and he had not ruled. Nothing could come over that water to change what a ruling would cost, and nobody could go over it to pay, until this boat swam." One duplicate date trimmed (7:11: "since the two hundred and sixty-eighth" → "in three days"). The scene's last line no longer repeats the ruling: "Cael turned at last, and looked at the far bank. He saw no honest way to give a ruling while it stood empty." (7:51). The launch is not thinned.

