## Reader Standard

The movement passes the substantive thirteen-year-old gate. There is no profanity, obscenity, blasphemous oath, sexual content, innuendo, or gore. Violence carries fear and bodily consequence without relish. Moral goodness is active and costly: Cael refuses powers that would endanger a child or expose other people's secrets; Lira accepts the difference between use and recklessness; Oryn observes consent even when Vastin nearly falls; Vastin names his theft and prior complicity as wrong; Brom and the ferry crowd help without converting help into status.

The remaining concern is one-pass **conceptual** comprehension, not vocabulary difficulty. Chapter 10's institutional explanation and chapter 12's document handling need especially clean distinctions for an audiobook listener. The prose should not be made still shorter to solve this.

