## B. Recomposed

**B6. The page is a copy (12:49–63).** Cut: the old brown stock, the binder's holes, the knife-cut edge, "I cut it… I took a blade to it", the inquiry into an archivist, "He had named a wrong as a wrong", and the *Edge?* prompt. **Now:** "There was one sheet in it, folded twice. It was of the plain paper that the Compact's house gives out to its desks, and it was written from head to foot in a square upright hand, in an ink that had not had time to fade." (12:51) · "It is a copy… The line stands in a record that is older than the section it is filed in, in a room where I had the right to read and no right to copy. I copied it. I set down the word and the place exactly as they are written there, letter for letter, and what stands round them, so that nobody need take my account of where the line sits." (12:53) · "It is your hand," said Karis. / "It is. I have no better warrant to give you…" / "I shall write that it is a copy, and whose… Then I shall read it with the same care as if it were not." (12:55–59) · the schedule "a careful man had copied… in order to show where one line fell" (12:61) · "It broke into the middle of an entry and out again, between two strokes of the pen, and he had copied the strokes." (12:63). The protected line stands exactly, once, at 12:65. Karis's finding and "It is not on the page before it, either… I read the pages on both sides" stand. The smaller honest beat kept is the one the brief allowed (the right to read and no right to copy); no history is minted for him. Also 12:237 "in brown ink" → "in a square black copy".

**B7. The layer: four facts (10:139–179).** Cut: the frame shop, the mender in the apron, the first-year rule, the damp frame and its shim, the office's schedules, "It has been attempted, more than once", Karis's account of how she had it, "I did not expect to have it confirmed by a man in an apron", and "That agrees with what I had…". **Now:** Vastin says only that he asked ("I asked to be shown what the office holds upon the thing that lies beneath the function", 10:143) and what he was shown ("The office has one page upon the matter, and it is the office's own. The page says that the layer is not to be opened; and it gives as its reason that it cannot be.", 10:159; "This one reserves no leave to anybody… A lock is a thing that somebody has the fellow to.", 10:163). Karis's protected line is whole, once, and unglossed (10:145): after it only "Karis had not lifted her voice. Her pencil had stopped, and she added nothing." `"Not *will not*. *Cannot.* There is no key."` is whole (10:155). **Two consequence anchors, in plain mouths:** Lira, "So there's nobody to ask… I've thought ever since Ostrand that there was a room somewhere, up a stair, with a person in it who knew what that thing was and wasn't saying… You're telling me that it's empty." (10:167); Brom, "Then it's no good anybody knocking at them… If a person wants to know what's under a plate, it'll not be the people who own the plates that tell him." (10:171). The inquiry is still spoken turn by turn; nothing is summarised. The scene is 828 words (was 1,039).

**B8. The anomaly column (11:237–261).** *Total* is the entry, not a sum. **Now:** "Its printed head was in plain letters, and it said what the office kept that column for: the anomaly. Karis went down it on the first sheet… and then she lifted a corner in the lowest row of the six, which was the oldest, and looked there. It was the same on every sheet. In every clerk's hand, in every one of six years, that column held a single word, and nobody had ever written a second word beside it." / *Total.* (once, set apart, 11:239) / "She turned the sheet so that the table could read it, and nobody asked her what it meant. Five of the seven people in that room had carried a sigil over such a line, and had felt it go out like a lamp. They did not need a clerk to tell them how much of it went." / "One word," said Lira. "For six years." / "It is all that they had a column for," said Karis. Nobody offers a theory. Karis's count on her fingers and `"More than four," said Karis.` stand (11:247–251). Cut: the clerk's figure, Cael's eyes not being equal to it, and "two numbers and no finding". When Lira asks: "I shall not say a number tonight. I have turned one row of six, by a lamp, and I have counted signs that I cannot read by telling one group from another. I should not put my name to such a count… I shall say how many when I have checked it in daylight." (11:259).

**B9. The technician's sheet (12:101–157).**
- *The date is never printed.* The marker is gone. Lira reads the sheet out from the meal-sacks; between the two protected lines, which stand exactly (12:111, 12:115), is her own "Then there's a date… Clerk's figures. A year of the registry's, and a day in it." (12:113). Karis still copies "the office's own figures down exactly as they stood" (12:133). No figure is on the page anywhere in the movement.
- *Where the sheet belongs.* The lid pocket is kept as where he carried it: "It did not lie there. I put it there to keep it out of the wet." (12:101); "The office had filed this one with the surveys, at the end of the second row, since it had no other shelf for it; and I should be glad if it went back there when you have done." (12:107). After the copy: "She slipped the tape of the uppermost row and lifted that row out whole, and slid the technician's sheet in at the foot of the row beneath, and laid the first back upon it. 'The second row, at its end,' she said." (12:139–141). The cutaway agrees (8:263: "in the flat's lid, where he had put it out of the wet… It had a date in it").
- *The definition is cut.* Brom still asks; Vastin: "I do not know which event he meant… The sheet does not say; and whatever would have said it is under that bar." (12:127). Also trimmed from his account of a technician's return: "a few men whose business is the function… the only people in the Compact who are sent as far down as that".
- *Movement 3 is not foreclosed.* "Cael had it the wrong way up and a table's length off, and he could see no more than that there were four short lines, and that something black lay across the last of them." (12:109). Cut: "which he had never learned to turn into days". He can read a registry date ("a thing that he had been reading upside down, on other people's desks, since his Kindling") and has chosen not to go and read this one.
- *The tracked passage (old 12:137–153) is re-entered, not reworded.* New order: Karis checks her copy and lays the sheet back → the lamp smokes, Brom says so, Lira pinches the wick → Cael looks for Seln and does not find him where he was → Karis asks him a practical question about the date's form → "I have read it," (12:149), before a word about stillness → Cael goes back over it: the others were looking at the bar; Seln's eyes were on her pencil, and he stopped when it came to the figures → "It was the date, then, and not the stripe." → the scene ends on a thing declined: "He stayed where he was. There was one paper in that room which he had not let himself touch all evening, and he did not go looking for a second." Gone: the definition of his usual stillness, the look, and the list of other unanswered things. Required facts kept: he went still at the date; he does not say the sum; nobody connects it to anyone; Cael does not know what it was.
- Consequences elsewhere: Karis no longer compares the sheet with her copy in the last scene (the sheet is back in its row); she writes under her copy "where the sheet itself now lay, and who had laid it there" (12:271). The inventory of the table reads "a courier's leather with six rows in it, and a barred sheet at the foot of the second, and beside the leather a broker's paper" (12:231).

**B10. Custody (12:173, 12:299).** Receipt, **before:** "…the sheets not yet counted, and one broker's report; one leaf cut out of a register; one technician's sheet, copied this night. Examined by nobody. — K." **After:** "*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*" Log, **before:** "…six years of an office's counting… one line, cut out of a register… Karis has all three and has read none of them." **After:** "…six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet." Karis's last speech: "an office's count that I cannot read… a box with…" → "six years of an office's sheets under that same index, and a title that I have never met, and a box that I have ruled round nothing" (12:295).

**B11. The broker's report (11:15; 12:11).** 11:15, **before:** "It had this town's name on it, and five others." **After:** "It had this town's name on it, and a board's; and where a crew's name should have stood, it had five strokes, and a count of six… I knew of only one five who had gone that way." 12:11, **before:** "…a town on no registry map, and a board, and five names." **After:** "…a town on no registry map, and a board; and where a crew's name should have been there were five strokes, and after the strokes a count, which was six." "That's us… In a folder." stands. Consistent with 10:55, where he has only two of their names, and those from a file.

**B12. The plate on the bluff (8:181).** **Before:** "He had once had a boy stood on one, on a bluff, and watched it stutter and go dark; and when the form came round that would have stood the boy there a second time, he had declined." **After:** "He had once carried a plate up a bluff, with a file and a set of questions, to a boy, and all three had told him nothing; and when the form came round that would have had the boy read a second time, he had declined." This is Book 5 ch58:229 and no more. 11:91 already says only that he "sat four feet from him for three days, asking", and that he declined when a form "would have stood Cael on a plate a second time"; I left it.

**B13. Chapter 7's opening.** Added at 7:7, before the first spoken word: "He had his back to the far bank, and he was aware of it the whole time, as a man is aware of a door behind him. Three nights ago a small pale woman had asked him, at a board, to go back over that river, to a town inside the Line with a gate and a book in it. He had said that he would rule, and he had not ruled. Nothing could come over that water to change what a ruling would cost, and nobody could go over it to pay, until this boat swam." One duplicate date trimmed (7:11: "since the two hundred and sixty-eighth" → "in three days"). The scene's last line no longer repeats the ruling: "Cael turned at last, and looked at the far bank. He saw no honest way to give a ruling while it stood empty." (7:51). The launch is not thinned.

