# Editorial review — Movement 002: No Sanction

## Review status and coverage

**Recommendation: targeted repair required, then recheck the changed lines and the chapter 11–12 join.** The movement is structurally strong and canon-clean, but chapter 12 still contains an unresolved coordinator placeholder inside a protected record. That prevents closure.

This is an **informed simulated editorial read**, not a blind cold read and not a real human audience test. It covers all six supplied chapters, chapter 7 through chapter 12, in order: 29,899 prose words. Canon, plan, protected-wording evidence, the supplied formula measurements, and the supplied source-overlap result were available.

## Unscored reader response

Interest catches immediately in the thaw-swollen river and the scow launch, then rises again in Lira's two-handed bout. The ferry accident is the movement's strongest sustained sequence: its physical problem is legible, each response follows from planted knowledge, and the passenger's calm use of the strap reveals Vastin through conduct before his name arrives. The long table then converts that physical credibility into moral credibility.

Interest slips slightly during the middle of the long explanation of the plate, function, layer, schedules, citation, and archive in chapter 10, and again while the three documentary objects are distinguished in chapters 11–12. The sentences are easy at the word level, but the institutional concepts accumulate faster than a thirteen-year-old listener can always sort them on one hearing. Brom and Lira help by restating stakes in ordinary language; a little more of that anchoring would help.

Vastin matters most in this movement. His problem is not merely that he has lost rank; it is that he has surrendered the only work by which he knew what to do with his hands. Lira remains the emotional anchor because her arm story turns recovery into choice rather than cure. I expect Cael to call for hands at the plank, take the road toward Treswick, and make Vastin find a place without admitting him as crew. I would continue.

