6. `manuscript/chapter-10.md`
   old: `"He told me what every mender is told in his first year, and it took him as long as it takes to wipe his hands. There is a thing beneath the plate which is not the office's work. A mender services the housing, and the frame, and the function that sits upon the reading, and he does not go below. I believed him the more readily for a thing that I had watched him do in that shop some while before. A river station had sent in a frame that read a fraction slow on wet mornings. There was nothing the matter with it; it was reporting the damp, and its fraction never varied, but the station did not care to be told about the damp. So he put a slip of brass into the housing, and it left off telling them, and he wrote in his note that they now had a worse frame. He knew that nobody would read the note." Vastin's eyes had gone to the window. "A man who will write that, knowing it will lie unread, does not trouble to deceive me about what is under a plate. I asked whether it was so in our house's frames only, or—"`
