### New canon minted (author's list)
- **The ferry's rig (flag).** The scow hangs from the cable by two running blocks, one over her bow and one over her stern, each on **a strap of plaited hide as long as a man** to a post at her rail. In slack water she crosses "set a little aslant on her two straps, so that the current itself shouldered her along the cable"; the hauling rope from her bow turns through a block lashed to **an oak post at the landing's head** and so into the shed. The drum has **an iron dog** (a pawl) hinged to the shed's king-post. The ferryman had two hauling ropes "before the snow"; the second, sent across by a skiff when the first strands, is the sixty yards owed. The old slip's **sill** lies under the mud two strides south of the post. In flood the cable bellies to the south; she crossed light in three minutes and came home in twenty-two. The rope has "two yarns gone where she bore on the sheave". The new stern strap is rove out of the tail of the stern line. The fare is a copper a head.
- **The first boat (flag):** first light on the 277th; eight passengers (a man with a ledger who carries the bag; two carters; a woman and a child; a drover; a lad; Vastin) and no goods; **the cordage stays on the western landing**. The bag had eleven pieces and none for the five. After noon she goes over again with twelve carters on foot, the bag and Ephram's letter; still no wheels.
- **The week (flag):** seven fires on the 271st, nineteen and thirty carts on the 276th. The river up a foot between the 268th and the 271st; the last of the shelf on the 275th; rain on the night of the 275th; water over the landing's lowest plank on the 276th; down a hand on the night before the boat and another by noon.
- **The mule's stool (flag):** a three-legged milking stool from the keeper's kitchen, set at the board's end on the 271st with the sixth bowl. Oryn will not sit on it; Lira names it; Vastin asks whether the place is taken and is told whose it is.
- **Seln's account of the gate (flag):** a man in the gate asks each person "for a name, and where the name is registered", and writes it; "every counter in every district keeps a list", read against each name as it is written; **the man who writes the name is the man who serves the paper.** What it holds: Lira's and Karis's certificates, kept for a slip, pending ("You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract"); Brom's Copper and the unsigned finding, "frozen as they stand"; and of Cael, "the thing that it holds is you… You would be produced", to a frame. Seln is not one of the four ("I was employed"). **Vastin, from the inside (11:75–79):** the instrument is drawn on the code's fourteenth chapter, ninth part; it does not wait upon a frame and does not lapse; he could not have lifted it when he held the seal; nothing lifts it "short of the settling of his status; and his status can be settled only on a plate."
- **Oryn at Lowmarch (flag):** her bench is a form set along the wall under the eave, a yard past the right-hand post of Pike's plank, by the door of the keeper's wash-house, where she works with the door open. Nine on the first morning. She takes payment in kind (a cheese). "I'll not spend on what grease will do." She does not go up the bank to the older healer.
- **The new cloth (flag):** Pike paints a new cloth at the thaw, with a hog's-bristle brush and a black of his own making; each crew's season figure is carried over and the cloth hangs in the order of the figures; "A hold reads down from the top until it comes to a crew that it can pay." The five have no figure, "So I carry the place": eleventh on the new cloth. Teague is first.
- **Teague (flag):** the wrist was unbound on the 272nd, a hundred and twenty-four days after it was bound. He has forty sacks of salt for the first boat and sees it over himself; then the block on the second ledge of the cold road. He did not put a day in his term; if the six are gone when he comes down, Cael is to "leave it with [Pike] which way". The tall man has put the steel back on his pole.
- **The bout (flag):** the 273rd, in the silent yard's square; a stave a hand taller than Lira against Cael's short one; 2–3 to her. Her left hand comes a beat behind her right. The arm "told" her once, in the third exchange, at a square block. She fell beside it, not on it.
- **Ephram's letter (flag), sent on the 277th:** *Ephram. You lose because you run it alone. I lost to a seven-foot pole all the cold term, three to none and then three to one, and each time somebody on a bench told me what I'd done before I had wiped the snow off. Find a bench. Tell Rooke the yard out here is quieter too. He'd not say it was worse. — C.*
- **Vastin as he arrives (flag):** a drover's coat of brown frieze, too short in the sleeve, bought "at the last market inside the Line"; the grey one left on its hook; an ash stick cut out of a hedge; a pack with one strap mended in cord; a small purse on a cord; the flat carried under the pack against his back and the leaf inside his coat in oilcloth. **The knee** "had begun to complain at about the hundredth mile and had left off being civil at the two hundredth"; no side is given. He can do ten miles in a day on it. He left "the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it". He crossed the sill at the last station on the 265th and reached the western landing on the evening of the 276th. His left hand would not open after the strap and has not lain flat since. He has paid a copper for the fare, a copper and a half for a loaf (a copper and a cut half), a copper for a bowl and a copper for the loft. He sleeps in **the loft over the tap, up the keeper's stair** (a copper the night).
- **What Vastin says of his own road to the papers (flag).** After the borrowed room he asked the house's frame-shop mender what lay under the function: "There is a thing beneath the plate which is not the office's work… he does not go below", a rule of the shop told to every mender in his first year. The office that has the charge of the stations keeps schedules for the housing, the plate, the frame and the function; "Below the function it has a single page. The page says that the layer is not to be opened, and it gives as its reason that it cannot be." It "has been attempted, more than once, by the people whose business it is". In the oldest register of housings that he was let to see, the line for the layer has no maker's name but a citation. **He cut that leaf out of a bound register with a knife, and says that it was wrong.** He could not go to the archive: an archive on the Line keeps a book at its door. The completion report reached him as a courtesy copy with no routing note; it took him the cold term to find the file it belonged to, "which stands in no register"; the abstracts "went up every year by seat courier, and… were received by nobody". He does not know who had them held, and looked. He does not know whether the copy was sent to him on purpose (Seln's question; left open).
- **The flat (flag):** a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather, "as broad as a man's shoulders and no thicker than two fingers", stamped *BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND.*; six rows, a year to a row, the newest uppermost, each in a tape; a pocket in the lid. **The abstracts:** printed forms; four signs and a number at the head (the Stair's sheet has the dead surveyor's four signs and thirty-two); a column for the day in the office's reckoning; **a measure given in lengths, knots and strides**; a narrow right-hand column with a stroke or none on each line, its heading in the unread signs, and *Total* with a figure at the foot of the row's last sheet. Karis counts more than four different groups of signs in the newest row. **No sheets are counted and no figure is given.**
- **The page (flag):** one leaf of thick brown stock with a binder's holes down its left edge and a cut right edge; a schedule of housings, each with a number, a date of setting and a mender's mark; the protected line breaks into one entry "in the same brown ink and the same hand"; the entry goes on to "a housing on the coast". Nothing about its age is said.
- **The sheet (flag):** thin stock, newer than the rest, a printed head, "a quick sloping hand"; a **technician's return**: the stations' office keeps a few men "whose business is the function", who must write up on the day anything they were not sent to find. The bar is as broad as a thumb, laid on both sides, cracked in drying. Vastin has seen such a bar "on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years" and never with initials.
- **Karis's receipt (flag), 12:169:** *Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, the sheets not yet counted, and one broker's report; one leaf cut out of a register; one technician's sheet, copied this night. Examined by nobody. — K.* He carries it where the leaf was.
- **Names given at the board (10:57–77):** the six tell Vastin their names; he had Cael's and Karis's from paper and "took some trouble never to learn" the others. Seln gives his.
- **Roles (all unnamed):** the courier with the cut hat-brim and the old carter with pale eyes (both from Book 7's road); the boy with a tally stick on the western landing; the landing broker and his clerk; the man with the ledger who carries the bag; the passengers; the woman with the loaves; the keeper; the frame-shop mender (from Book 6).

### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
**Calendar.** The night of the **two hundred and seventy-seventh day past the Line**, at Lowmarch. Days on the page: 271, 272, 273, 275, 276, 277; Vastin's window, 265–276. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page.

**Where everyone is.** Seven under the lower inn's roof: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln; Oryn, who is on her road and at her bench by first light; and **Vastin, in the loft over the tap, at a copper a night**, who is on nobody's sheet and has asked for nothing but a bed. Teague's five are at the upper inn, with forty sacks of salt on the bank. The brown mule is in the ferryman's shed.

**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: thirty** (the read on the ground at the landing, the 277th). Shadow used twice in all and shut; held at dusk on the 272nd and the 277th. Anchor's counted uses still two. Tide: Oryn twice, himself three times, nobody else; he did not ask to read the knee. **The still place** has not been read, touched or named. He has not touched the cord or either brass edge for a hundred and nine days; the ruling is *hold*. His near sight went at the landing and was back for print by the afternoon. **He has not ruled.** He said on the 271st that he would rule when the first boat was in, and on the night of the 277th that he would say it "at the plank in the morning, in daylight" and ask for hands. He has not touched the leaf; his hand lay a hand's width from it for the whole of a lamp. He lost the bout to Lira, two touches to three. He has written to Ephram. He kept back his own question to Vastin (why the second reading was declined) and does not know the answer. He has told Vastin nothing of what he carries.

**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Left arm: the eighth day out of the wool on the 273rd. It told her once, in the bout, and she did not stop.** It is slow; she has woven a hurdle with it, tied a trace with both hands, and been pulled to her feet by it; it ached after rain. She did not fall on it. Right ankle sound in the great boot. Her certificate is in her coat; she does not know any longer what she would be holding it for. She still has Oryn's crossbow and its one bolt (not mentioned in these chapters). She sat last at the table and asked "Why us."

**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No declaration; no grey at the landing. **Right shoulder still in the older healer's webbing**; she pressed the knot on the 272nd and went away; "it isn't when." He carried a blocking timber on the left shoulder and shored the post. He has the purse; a hundred and eighteen. He roped the three boxes for carrying on the night of the 277th, "whichever road they go up".

**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Her case stands as she made it and is "not by a line" altered. She has, received and receipted, **the flat (six rows, the sheets not counted, one row turned over once), the completion report, the leaf and the technician's sheet**, and her own exact copy of the sheet with a ruled box and *not read* in it. She has said that the word on the leaf is a title and not a name, that she has never met it in any stratum, and nothing more. She has said "More than four" and will not say how many more. She cannot read the index; it is now an office's index and not only the dead man's. She has not connected anything to anything. She keeps the lamp.

**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He has spoken with Vastin for the first time (the doorway) and has given him his name. He has stopped his count at **forty-seven notches on the second wand (a hundred and forty-seven days)** and laid the wand on Karis's table. He went still at the date on the technician's sheet and said only "I have read it." He has taught nobody anything. He thinks the completion report may have been sent to Vastin on purpose and cannot tell. **The case is on him, shut.**

**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. A bench under the eave since the 272nd. She saw the bout and said nothing. She has not asked about the knee, and Vastin has offered it to nobody. **She has not read Cael.** She has shut her mouth three times on the same sentence. She did not sit on the stool. She does not know that Seln is Shadow's source.

**Vastin.** No office, no seal, no rank; he left the seal in a tray "some months" ago and walked three hundred miles. **No age is stated.** A bad knee; raw hands; a left hand that will not lie flat. **No Iron Wall has been shown or named**; he held the ferry's stern strap with his hands and his back. He has handed over three things in his own order and holds Karis's receipt. He has said: that he holds no office and has no sanction; that the layer cannot be opened ("There is no key"); that the one paper which should name its maker cites a title and sends its reader to Treswick's lower archive; that nothing of his follows him and the Stair's rim is not his; that the pending paper is unchanged; why he did not take it to a council. He knows the six by name. **He does not know** the mechanism, what Cael carries, the instruments' behaviour in Cael's hand, Karis's case or the four sites (beyond hearing Brom say "four on the hide" and Lira name the Stair), who held the abstracts, or what is under the bar. He has not weighed the sheet's date. He has not read the flat's last row through, and nothing has been said of a directive or an objection. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. He has had nothing to do since noon.

**Teague and his crew.** The wrist is unbound; he has tried it on nothing. He asked "When?" and was told "I don't know." His term holds without a day in it. The tall man drove the wedge at the post and grounded his spear for Lira; he, the bow and the older healer have said nothing to the six. Teague looked at Vastin's hands a stride longer than a crew looks, and said nothing. Nobody in his crew is named. He knows nothing of the mechanism or of what was said at the table.

**Pike's book and the cloth.** **Eleven lines.** The season line's number still blank. **Eleventh on the new cloth**, painted on the 272nd. Vastin is "nothing yet" and is not entered; the boat's saving was "said". Oryn is on no slip and Pike has no column for her.

**Objects and money.** **A hundred and eighteen marks in hand**, unchanged. The reserve sealed. Owed: **sixty yards of four-strand rope** to the ferryman (the cordage is on the western landing; his one hauling rope has two yarns gone). **The hurdle is paid** (woven on the 272nd, hung on the 276th). **The letter to Ephram is sent.** The three boxes are at the hearth's end, roped. Stake thirty-two has still never been driven.

**The Quieting, as known.** Unchanged in the field. On paper: an office has surveyed the sites for six years by cord, has classed each under four signs, and has kept a narrow column and a total; its abstracts were held where nobody received them; the count is more than four.

**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before. The anomaly of the cord and the edges: the six. Karis's case: the six. What Vastin brought: the six and Vastin. Nobody has said who made the layer, or why, or what is running in it. Nobody has connected the sheet's date to anybody.

**Words said aloud for the first time.** *Vastin* (Seln, at the landing). *Treswick* by Vastin, at noon, from the other side. On paper only, and said by nobody: the title on the leaf. Not said aloud by anybody in chapters 10–12: *west*.

**Open threads.** A ruling to be given at the plank in the morning. A leaf that Cael has not touched. A date that stopped Seln. A bar with something under it. A figure under *Total* that does not agree with a count. A paper that came to a desk with no routing note. A sentence that a healer has swallowed three times. A knee that nobody has asked about. A man in a loft with a receipt and nothing to do. A rope on the wrong bank. A captain's term with no day in it. A question that Cael kept.

