editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:109:"Two," said Brom. "Teague's after the thaw as well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:259:The fifth of them was bent under a mule's belly with both hands on a girth. He had a band of black leather laced on his right wrist; Pike had guessed him a Bronze once, under that eave, and had never been told. Cael put up one hand to that bent back, open, as carters did on this side of the Line when they passed on a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:267:Teague's five went up toward the cold road in file, a crew's length apart, and not one of them looked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:15:"You're the five for the fence. Teague said one of you was tied up." His eyes went from a sling to four yards of webbing. "I count two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:25:The hillside rose behind the byre, steeply, for four hundred yards, to a band of grey rock that ran across it like the coping of a wall. The fence left the yard at the gate, and climbed that hill aslant, and then ran east along the foot of the rock. Teague's posts stood out of the snow the whole way up from the gate, six strides apart, as straight as a ruled line, for as far as he had got. Beyond the last of them a row of flat stones went on across the slope in the same line, each the size of a hearth-loaf, with a wisp of straw showing at its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:She had Seln pace it along the outside of the standing fence. From the yard gate to the nearest grey back it came to three hundred and fifty yards, which was the distance at which she had once lit a fire under a ledge and seen nothing stir. Brom and the mule dragged the factor's hay sledge up the hill to the last of Teague's posts, and Lira sat on it with the bow across her knees. She had seventeen heads before her there, a hundred and fifty yards off, and the whole of the slope beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:73:He climbed beside Teague's posts and did it again at two hundred yards, and again at sixty, a long way beyond her, where he could see the lichen on the nearest animal's plates. At sixty the read told him nothing whatever about the herd, since its whole world was a pace and a half across. It told him about the slope under his boots. He shut it, and looked back along the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:233:They began on the posts at first light on the hundred and forty-first, and it was Brom's work from the first hole to the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:235:He stood at the end of Teague's run before they lifted a stone, and looked back along the standing posts with one eye shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:239:He took that line off Teague's posts and carried it on across the hill. It went as slowly as anything goes that is done properly in frozen ground. A cap-stone came up, and the straw came out of the hole beneath it, dry, since the frost had never got in; then Brom walked nine feet of ash upright into its place against his good shoulder, and Seln and Cael drove split stone down round its foot with the maul until it rang, and rammed gravel on top. Lira sat astride a rail at the standing end, sighting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:243:They set fourteen that day, and fifteen on the next, and twelve on the hundred and forty-third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:245:It was on the second afternoon that Cael saw Seln at the other end of the fence, with a length of twine stretched from the gatepost to the last of Teague's posts, walking along it with his head bent. Somewhere about the middle he stopped, and stood before two posts for the time that he would have given to a paper. Then he coiled his twine, and came all the way back along the hill, and said something to Brom that took perhaps eight words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:261:Seln was looking at the hill. He had told one person, and he had chosen which. Cael understood why it had not been the one with the book; a thing that went into that book against a man's name would stay there, and Seln had spent fifteen years watching what became of names in books. Teague had dug forty-one holes for somebody else's posts before the frost. Two of his own stood a hand out of true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:265:They pinned the far side on the hundred and forty-fourth, with the herd mowing a grass tongue a furlong above them, and on the next morning the factor walked his line from the gate to the last rail. He kicked six posts. He counted the new ones with his tally stick, twice, as a man counts who has been cheated before; and then he looked up the hill at seventeen grey backs, for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:279:Pike said their line at dusk on the hundred and forty-sixth, in a room with thirty people in it and a fire that a man could have stood in. He gave it out as he gave everything, with his shoulder turned: a fence, at the axe-head hold, the long one; forty-one posts set and the far side pinned; a herd shifted off the line and nothing run; witnessed. He dipped his pen for the last column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:3:Teague came down to the lower inn at dusk on the hundred and forty-seventh day, and stopped a stride from their end of the hearth, with his thumbs in his belt over the hand-axe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:23:"What it buys me. You came out of three of them." Teague's eyes went to a sling, and to four yards of webbing, and came back. "I'd not say that you came out cheap."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:Nobody at that fire could dispute it, and Cael went over the offer as he went over any slip. Inside a line the other four were exactly what this man had said of his own; they had fought there as such, three times, with a bill and a knife and a borrowed bow, and two of them sat here bound up in consequence. He himself was another matter, which Teague did not know. But he had never once stood up against anybody with the ten fragments shut, and holding a thing down on a road was not the same as going without it in a fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:29:"Three touches. A touch is what the one who took it calls, since I've nobody to judge. Over the pegs is a touch." Teague drew one thumb out of his belt. "And nobody strikes what a healer's had her hands on this season. That's her arm, and his shoulder, and my bow's hip. And your left side. I saw the tape on you at a river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:33:All five went up. Teague looked along them, and nodded by the width of a finger, and went back up the bank to his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:By the next morning there was a square trodden in the snow under the great ash, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner. Somebody had carried a bench down from the upper inn and set it along the western edge. Lira took one end of it. Brom took the middle, since a shoulder in webbing was of no use in a square. At the other end, with her staff across her knees and a splinting board at her feet, sat a lean old woman in a cap, who looked at the knot on Brom's webbing as she sat down and at nothing else that belonged to the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:45:Teague made the pairs himself on the hundred and forty-eighth morning, and he made the first of them without seeming to think.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:49:There was a bundle of sticks in the snow by the bench, cut to every length, and Cael chose a stave of ash as long as his arm, since the only steel that he owned was a knife. The tall one took a pole. It was seven feet of the same wood, as thick as a wrist, with a wad of rag bound over one end; and its owner carried it out to the middle of the square as that spear had been carried on every road that Cael had seen it on, upright, and grounded at each stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:61:He was aware by then of what his own feet were doing. Wind was in them, as it had been since a painted floor, and it had leaned him toward the first stride of everything for longer than he had known Teague's name. It leaned now, hard, away to the left, with the whole of a burst ready behind it. He told it no; and with the no there came a thing that he had not expected, which was that his feet had nothing of their own to offer in its place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:63:The read asked next, civilly, as it always did. A pace and a half would have had that pole from the moment that its butt left the snow: its weight, and which way the weight was going, a full beat before his eyes could tell him. It did not feel like doing anything. Not a person in that meadow would have known. Teague had said *not a sigil*, and Cael had no sigil, and never had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:81:"That's the three," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:89:"I do not fight in yards," he said, when Teague's look came to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:91:"No. I'd not have thought it." Teague did not press him. He had found out in an inn what that one was, and he left him at his peg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:On the night of the hundred and forty-eighth Teague sat at the upper inn's fire with his right arm laid along the old woman's board, and let her unlace him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:159:The leather came off first, which had been on that wrist since before the boy could lift a pack. Under it the skin was as white as a root, and the joint was thicker than its fellow by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:169:"Leave it shut till the thaw, and I'll hand you a wrist," she said. "Use it on that road and I'll hand you this board, and you'll walk up to your bearing with your arm on a plank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"Then you've a spear, and a bow, and a boy with a back like a young ox, and three iron bars. And there's a herd lying up over that ledge, by what the boy saw. Shift it as a carter would." She began to lay the webbing. "I've no Path that knits. I've told you so every year, and every year you've thought I'd find one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:177:"Say it to the wrist."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:195:Teague looked at him. He was all wrists, and he ate as a fire eats; he carried more than his share on every stage and had to be stopped. He went at each thing a half-beat before he had seen the whole of it, and that would have to be got out of him by somebody before it was got out of him by a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:197:It was not a thing that a captain liked, to send one of his own to another crew's bench to be taught, and Teague priced it as he priced any stage. It would cost him nothing in marks. What it would cost him was the boy's seeing, every morning for a cold term, that there was a thing which his own captain could not show him. He had known captains who would not pay that; their people stood in the lower half of Pike's cloth, or had left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:209:"You'd go to whoever had it," said Teague. "You came to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:It was the fourth night that he had seen it: two before he went up the cold road, and two since. He had not gone looking; the house on the rise had one window on that side, and a man who has kept a watch on roads for as long as he had does not sit with his back to a window. The light came on after dark and burned for an hour or two, and for the whole of that time a small still shape stood between two hurdles, facing outward. It was the quiet one. Teague had needed two looks to find him, once, in a room. He found him now in one, and took no credit for it, since the man was not trying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:221:He did not go down the bank. A man who goes and looks through a hurdle at what he has been shut out of has told the other crew what he is worth. There was a bench by the lower inn's hearth, and the boy knew where Teague sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:The cold road paid. He had salt work from now to the thaw, and more of it if the pass stayed shut. The bearing paid nothing; the bow had said so twice, and had been right both times. But Teague had told fifty people at a hearth that his crew would come, and he had not said it for a price, and he would have held to it if he had said it to a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:277:Brom had begun to laugh, silently, with his bound shoulder held still. "Twice. And nobody touched you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:301:*Tide, from Oryn: asked, on the hundred and forty-eighth, for Lira's ankle. Told no. Not used. That is the rule working.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:25:Teague's four had come down by then. He glanced once at the bench, and at the young one walking a count with his tongue between his teeth, and he made his pairs as though he had seen nothing unusual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:43:"That's a touch taken," said Teague. "It's all you'll get by way of calling. One apiece."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:57:"Three, and one," said Teague. "You'd none the last time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:77:On the hundred and sixty-first day Cael was on the rope-house roof with a mouth full of nails. It was the job that Brom had put his hand up for, at five, and they had been at it on and off since the fence, whenever the frost let a shingle be split. Seln knelt at the ridge with a hammer. Brom stood on the ladder's fourth rung and passed shingles up left-handed, a dozen at a time, in a rope sling. Below them, at the shed's door, Lira had the brown mule by the cheek-strap and was telling her what she thought of a beast that ate a ferryman's straw and did nothing for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:89:It lay coiled against the drum, as thick as Cael's wrist. He took the free end over his shoulder, and a bundle of shingles under his arm, since they were what he had in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:3:They came down to Thornwater's ford in the forenoon of the two hundred and sixty-fifth day, and Cael did not at first know the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:65:She had not asked after that animal in a hundred and forty-three days, by letter or by carter, and she did not ask now. She was a Tide, of the fourth rank in Iron, and nobody at that gate had ever asked her for the figure either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:77:She tied off the linen, and sent the old man away with a word about keeping it dry, and only then got up and turned to them. Her eyes passed over Cael as they would have passed over the gatepost. They went to four yards of webbing, and to a knot upon it that she had not tied; they went down to a boot with river water running out of its lacings; and they stopped at a fold of grey wool, and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:81:She had Lira on the bench on the two hundred and sixty-sixth morning, as soon as there was light to see by, with the frost still white on the flags.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:She got up, and walked round him at her stride and a half, and studied four yards of webbing from the top turn to the bottom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:177:"Four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from the iron to its middle," said Karis. "I had that by the bend, from outside. I have not one sheet of the place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:231:Cael counted the board, as he counted any room. There was a big man with one arm bound and six bowls laid; and a girl who kept turning her left hand over on the wood, as though it had come back to her from a mender's; and a small pale woman with a hide; and a quiet one with his back to the wall; and at the end, on her feet, a healer who had walked two days down a saddle because it was her road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:229:He learned something from each losing. But what he learned most from was a game that Teague set on the hundred and ninetieth day, with a sack of meal in the middle of the square and three people at either end. Whoever carried it over the other side's pegs had it. Cael stood with Seln and Karis, since they were the three of the five who had two arms apiece. He turned to them to say what he thought they should do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:Teague's three had not spoken, and their captain was not even among them. He stood by the bench with his thumbs in his belt. He had looked at a place on the trodden snow, once, before the word was given. When it was given the spear was standing in that place, and the bow was where the spear had been, and the boy had the sack over the pegs and was waiting to be told whether he might put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:"You put your hands up," said Teague afterward, without any unkindness. "It's a good way to choose a road. It's no way to cross a yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:267:Karis said the other thing on the two hundred and sixty-third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:39:  - day 112: Lowmarch; the Stair slip
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:40:  - day 113: Millrace; the Stair's line
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:41:  - day 114: the trap
