editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:55:- **Road times (prose, fixed):** Lowmarch ↔ Thornwater two days; Thornwater → the Fallow rise two days; Lowmarch → Oxhollow six days (north-west); Lowmarch → Millrace one day south on the Stair road; Millrace's fork → the Stair's line the same afternoon; the Stair → the Hall three days along the bearing; the Hall → the fork one day; the fork → the fourth site a day and a half; the fourth site → Lowmarch six days; the fourth site's gap → Thornwater four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:78:| 6 | Shadow-adjacent (Bronze) | Seln (edition B4 ch34) [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit C1] | Integrated; **sealed** | Presence-suppression and movement-masking. **Costs from waking; drifts at dusk.** Sealed from any witness who could carry it west (a defector's tradecraft in his hands would burn its source). **Book 7 uses it exactly twice:** at the Ring, unseen and unattributable to Oryn, and on the Stair ascent, unseen by the rim. [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit B2] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:117:| Book 6's faceless faction | Known to exist; unnamed, unlocated | Two reaches in this book (paper, day 90; in person, day 114) |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:128:- **No weapons of note** beyond knives, Brom's bill-hook and Lira's long knife. **No crossbow** until Oryn hands hers to Brom at the Ring's stone (day 96).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:142:| 129 | 132 | 96 | The inn's back room, six. Fallow: no price. Stair: 300 taken, never paid |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:168:| The Guilds Compact | File escalated; asset-restriction pending | Reaches across the Line twice through a landing broker's seal (the Fallow and Stair slips). Knows quiet ground; learns which crew walks out of it; **never learns that anything works inside** |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:169:| The faceless faction | Unnamed, unlocated | Six hooded on the Stair's rim, plus a second Anchor hand. No faces |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:185:- Objects and ledgers: boxes, case, kit, ledger, sheets, map, straightedges, crossbow and bolt count, mules; Pike's lines and wall position.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:382:- **Oryn, as seen (flag):** not tall, square in the shoulder, broad in the hand, hair cropped as short as a boy's; a man's brown coat made for a bigger person and taken in ("off a peg that nobody was using any more", given by a hold in her first year); a low hoarse voice; a small brown mule with a pack-saddle, two panniers and a crossbow in an oiled cover lashed on top. She stops a stride and a half from people. Cael puts her at about ten years older than himself; no age is stated. Her right heel is rubbed raw and taped, and has been for two rounds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:429:- Oryn has failed three surface readings of Cael, knows only that he heals fast with no readable current, knows nothing of fragments, has run no deep reading, keeps no written record, and has put one day on her route for the promised whole-and-still reading (`manuscript/chapter-20.md:317-351`; `manuscript/chapter-22.md:53-89,137-155,211-233`). Her mule still carries her panniers and covered crossbow; do not spend owner decision #24 early (`manuscript/chapter-20.md:143-159`; `OWNER-DECISIONS.md:103-107`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:523:- **Exact party/object placement:** six at the den; Brom wears the middle box; Seln is on one knee; Oryn's mule is hobbled west of the line stone with two panniers, six borrowed blankets, the unused left boot and covered crossbow; two boxes remain in Thornwater, with the reserve sealed in the third (`manuscript/chapter-27.md:13-29`; `manuscript/chapter-30.md:3-7,219-239`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:167,183`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:538:- **Shadow has been used ONCE and remains sealed.** Only Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln had the sightline; Oryn was screened and saw him arrive, not come; the pack is no witness. Oryn cannot identify Seln as its source. Seln struck Cael's second reason for silence ("I shall not be a reason… I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant") WITHOUT offering to be known as the source: Movement 6 tells her sources by Path, not by person, and the seal serves again at the Stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:539:- **The Ring:** nine hounds counted on the white; two dead by Brom's bolts (thirteen feet; six feet); seven away "north and south along the line's outside", direction unobserved. The crossbow was on the mule; Seln fetched it during the retreat round the floor and Brom spanned it by hand. Bolts: six, five in the sleeve from the 97th. Geometry as closed in Movement 4.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:554:- **Oryn's crossbow (flag):** has an iron stirrup at its nose and is spanned with a hook, which was in the near-side pannier and was not fetched; drawn by hand it cuts the fingers. It "throws low, a hand" at thirteen feet. A leather sleeve on the stock held **six** bolts. No history of the bow is given; Oryn says only that she looked at it at noon and left it, "a healer's reason".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:589:**Objects and money.** Board purse unchanged: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and eight in hand.** The Fallow hundred lies in Pike's box; no instruments were found; the posting (five strokes) is in Hesk's book. The crossbow, with **five bolts**, is with the crew; its spanning hook is in the mule's near-side pannier. One bolt lies on the Ring's floor. Six blankets returned, one a hand narrow. The rope is still owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:602:- **Brom/crossbow:** one hand has four mended string-lines; the restriction ends on day 100; the bow has five bolts, while the spanning hook remains in the mule's near pannier (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:15-21,53`; fixes 2–7, 9–11, 14, 16–17 and 19). The long knife is blunt from Blade's bill (`manuscript/chapter-33.md:211-213`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:626:- **Bodies:** Lira one-armed, shooting; Brom's string restriction ended on the 100th and the crossbow is on his belt; Cael whole. **New canon KEEP:** the founding stone from Ostrand's old gate; the hold's yard crossbow; the husband and the hold's boy (unnamed). **Names:** none minted. **Vastin:** one window, three sentences, no age.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:641:- **The yard bow (flag):** the hold's old stirrup crossbow, kept under the gate-walk in a goatskin "for the fox"; a belt hook; eight blunt bolts fletched with goose feather; it throws left. A straw sack on a stake outside the west gate, forty paces and then sixty, shot at noon. Lira's scores: none of eight in the ring (the 104th); two (the 106th); five from the rail and three from the knee (the 108th); four of eight from the rail and one of one from the knee at sixty (the 110th). The bowman's rail is worn hollow by thirty years of his left hand; nothing has come up to the white stone for fifty-seven evenings; he leaves Lira the walk at dusk on the 110th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:678:**Objects and money.** Three boxes; the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. **Oryn's mule, with the crew** (one empty pannier on the saddle). Hesk's satchel: the log (the rule, the note, the second pole, the birthday line), the old volume, the notebook, the letters, the broker's posting, Pike's lines on bark including the Fallow copy. The crossbow, hook and five bolts. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and two in hand** (six to the hold on the 102nd). Owed: the ferryman's rope; the Fallow hundred lies in Pike's box unpaid. Hesk's parcel has not come; no boat has come in from the west since the 91st.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:695:- **Equipment and money:** 102 marks in hand; three boxes and Oryn’s mule; Seln’s shut case; Oryn’s empty pannier left on the saddle while she carries the other and the spare rope; the yard crossbow, belt hook and five bolts travel with Brom; the ring sheets, bark slips, four chips, calfskin rings and charcoal round remain in Karis’s boxes (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:55–67,379`; `chapter-41.md:3–39`; `chapter-42.md:205–277`; `chapter-43.md:171–215`; `chapter-44.md:209–245`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:710:- **The rim:** six, faceless — no seals, names or office; four crossbows and two without, the second Anchor hand one of the six (not the Ostrand-road woman: the same craft, a different weight in the laying). They waited forty paces outside the edge. Their bows are left disabled; their bolts are too stout for Oryn's bow. The crew's five bolts stayed five. The Stair slip's three hundred was taken and never paid. The second paper was lodged sealed with the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:712:- **The Stair's chamber:** walls cut floor to roof in five courses, each as tall as a forearm; a line that is not on the Ring stands BETWEEN the courses, on its own, recurring "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride" — the Fallow's radius. (There is no continuous groove.) Karis paced the perimeter on the morning of the 115th and got two hundred. The open kit with its contents in taking-out order was found and is carried by Brom; **the case is untouched; the index and the stroke-and-bar are not decoded; nobody theorizes.** "Compact" is not said in this movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:715:- **Length:** the reads split; the coordinator ruled that the Stair's action is not cut for the map's 40,000 line, and repetition is removed instead. **Names:** none minted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:721:- **The ground at the Stair (flag; one picture for chapters 48–52).** A ridge runs north and south; the Stair road comes south along its top and drops off its western face. Halfway down, the slope eases into a brow, and there the carters' live ruts turn north and go the whole way round a shallow bowl some five hundred yards across. The edge of the Quieting lies forty paces inside the ruts. The old straight road, two grown-over ruts, runs on down from the brow, crosses the edge between two iron bars eight feet apart (the old one on the north side, the newer on the south, with Karis's stone between), and goes to the floor: a hundred and ninety yards of grass, then thirty of floor to the hole. The floor is a bare round plate sixty of Cael's strides across, with no upright thing on it. The hole is two strides square, at the middle; the first step is on its eastern side and the stair goes down westward, forty steps, cut and not laid, every edge unworn; the air changes at the twentieth. The chamber opens westward from the stair's foot: ten strides each way; its roof is the underside of the floor, a foot above Brom's head; dust "as thin as the bloom on a plum". The rim (the ruts) is two hundred and sixty yards from the hole and sixteen hundred paces and a little more round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:724:- **Hound sign at the Stair (flag):** old runs in the grass on the north side, unused since the frosts began; no den that Seln can find from outside. He says that he does not know why and will not guess. No stillhound appears in this movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:725:- **The six on the rim (flag).** Hooded, in road grey, each with a carter's dust-cloth across the face; no word, cry or call from any of them at any point. Four stations outside the edge: at the gate (the brow), *the gate bow* and, two yards south of him, *the empty-handed one*; on the south, *the south bow* and *the standing one*, who carries nothing and gives one sign at the end (an arm raised and let fall); *the west bow*, alone, who never looses; *the north bow*, alone. **Four crossbows among six people.** They lay up off the ruts until the five were over the stone (Seln walked the ruts at dusk and before light and found nothing on them).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:730:- **The boxes were not touched** by the six: Seln had laid a stem of grass under every knot. His reason: six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:742:- **Lira's bowls (flag):** "We lay six bowls… Brom puts six out every night and takes one back." / "I'll eat out of it. I'll not sit in a place." At the Stair she eats out of the last of the six, standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:756:**Calendar.** Late afternoon of the **hundred and fifteenth day past the Line**, at the stone on the eastern edge of the Long Stair, in full sun. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term", "frost", "the thaw", "the snow", "a dry month"). Days on the page: 111, 112, 113, 114, 115; in Seln's window, the night of the 112th; in Teague's, the 113th, 114th and the dawn of the 115th. Weather: hard frost every night, ringing roads, ice along Lowmarch's bank; no snow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:760:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-three**, by his own log (fourteen on the 113th; nine on the 114th; none on the 115th). **Shadow used twice in all** (the Ring; the Stair), unseen by anybody outside the circle who could carry it; it is shut again. Anchor: looked twice and unbound once. The tenth has now read one other person, with her leave, at one sitting; **he has still read himself once only**, and owes Oryn two more readings on different days and then all of it; she told him "not tonight" on the 113th. What he found in himself is unsaid and unnamed aloud. Body: the right forearm struck (bends on the 115th); a weal across the left palm, taped, unmended; hands warm again. He has said *Noted* twice to Teague. He holds the kit (Brom carries it). Hesk's satchel: the log, the old volume, the notebook, the letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark. Three letters still unanswered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:766:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. One point set, on her lamp, outside the stone on the night of the 114th. Holds: **forty sheets** (eleven of the Ring, twenty-nine of the Stair); **forty-one kinds, the same at both**, entered and underlined; **the line between the courses** (not on the Ring; on every wall of the chamber; recurring at ten cords and a piece, two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, the perimeter's radius), shown on the eastern wall's sheets; the Stair's perimeter table (the Fallow's round, to the stride); the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, written down from Seln's reading; the leaf of the trials, inside her coat; the calfskin, with *guess* struck out beside the Stair's ring and still standing beside the far south-eastern one; the Ternhall brass, out of its cloth and held over the hide. Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid. She has said that the floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size, and nobody took it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:772:**Teague.** He stood for the five at Lowmarch and sat at "eleventh". He hung the second paper on the nail. He took and forfeited a four-mark slip to be near; he saw one boy, outside the edge, declare a Wind, a fire and a third thing that he cannot name; he said only that he saw a man walk out of the Quiet declaring, and asked nothing. His three walked the ruts by lantern and brought in three crossbows. He held the rim through the 115th and lent a lantern. He still has a day's posts on the forty-mark fence. His crew is unnamed; the healer of no Path has not appeared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:776:**Objects and money.** Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and two in hand**, unchanged. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against the broker's mark, and will not be marked. The Fallow hundred lies there too. **The kit:** case, pacing cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, mallet; the ledger apart, with Seln. Four crossbows by the stone, strings cut and locks empty, one broken; the rim's recovered shafts in a row beside them (thirteen loosed, by the log of the 114th), useless to the healer's bow. Karis's lamp filled; Teague's lantern returned. Owed still: the ferryman's rope; three replies; Hesk's parcel has not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:778:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Eight lines**; the eighth said twice in the crew's hearing on the 112th. **Eleventh; Teague ten up.** The Stair paper carries five strokes and **HOLD, on Pike's own mark**, said to the room and to be said to the meadow. Pike has said that his book has no column for what the eighth line means.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:780:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. That the fragments answered at the Stair: the six. Which fragments, by Path: the six; where the ninth came from: the five. Teague and his three: a declaration outside the edge, of more than one kind. Pike and the room: that six went in at the Fallow and six came out. The registry's side: as above. Nobody has said why anything answers him inside. Nobody has put a verb between a made floor, an age with no ruler, the same round twice, a circle whose size the carvers knew, and a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:785:Movement 8's packet already carries the day-115 rim, bearing, two mules, six people, Brom mended once, Lira closed again and Teague turning back (`MOVEMENT-008.md:18–20`). Its author must additionally inherit these surviving-prose facts:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:789:- **Equipment:** Brom has Oryn's bow, hook and five unshot bolts; the four captured crossbows are disabled and their thirteen bolts do not fit it. There are three archive boxes, the crew's brown mule, Oryn's grey jenny, 102 marks, the recovered kit, and Seln's shut case (51:167–197; 52:303–359).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:790:- **Research custody:** Seln has the field ledger inside his coat; Karis has eleven Ring sheets plus twenty-nine Stair sheets, forty-one kinds, the trials leaf, the perimeter table, the calfskin with the Stair's *guess* struck and the south-eastern *guess* still standing, and the Ternhall straightedge (52:165–209,247–305,355–369).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:796:**Movement 7 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: the ten audit fixes kept and their joins smoothed; ch52's three tracked passages restaged (scene 2 re-entered on the bolt at the thirty-first step; the kit read from the cord inward); the design confirmed once; twice-told states removed from ch49–51 with the night log whole; the closing log's "five years ago" made to stand (the ledger's eight stations are the old irons, five cold terms by Seln; the stroke-and-bar is "two visits", the later undated; nothing decoded); a register pass. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES** — seven applied by the coordinator: **what Cael asked at Millrace's bench is corrected to the source's actual distinction** (leave to touch a boy's wrist while Oryn held it — refused; on the road he asks to read HER — granted, "a different question"), with the bench's count and the day's log to match; and ch52 says Karis reads the radius figure from scaled divisions within the lone line, not as a spacing along the wall. All three tracked passages RESOLVED, no new tracking. Gates 0; overlap as below. 42413 words (the length ruled by the coordinator: the Stair's action not cut for the map's 40,000 line). Author-sourced sections above refreshed from the post-repair report; where they still describe the bench request as a request to read Oryn, the Tide ruling above governs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:804:- **Deployment since the Line: TWENTY-SEVEN at book end.** Twenty-three after the Stair; then at the Hall on the 119th the read under water (24), Compression taken standing (25), an Anchor point laid by hand in the breach and held (26); and on the 120th Tide on Oryn, asked and granted, one sitting (27). The Anchor point set as a trial on the 118th is NOT counted ("I'll not call a trial a use"), which agrees with the protected inventory: Anchor used twice this year, once each way (the Stair's unbinding; the Hall's holding). Self-readings are not counted. **Shadow was not used**; it has been used exactly twice in the book and remains sealed; Oryn does not know Seln is its source.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:808:- **The sites and the line:** the Ring (the Fallow), the Stair, the Hall, and the fourth, taken as lying on one line "as near as this skin can show"; no compass bearing is claimed for the whole. Only two maker sentences exist outside protected text: "Someone made this pattern." (said once; nobody answers) and "a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one." Karis's hypothesis is still unstated; her window ends on the pencil stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:809:- **Sheets: fifty-two** (eleven Ring, twenty-nine Stair, twelve Hall). The survey kit is on the table at Lowmarch: the case in tarred canvas, a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, never driven, and a mallet; the dead man's ledger, shut, is Karis's (Seln gave it to her). The case of Seln's is shut and untouched; nothing is decoded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:811:- **Bodies:** Lira's left arm slung and holding nothing, her right ankle never mended; her order stands at first snow (Oryn "will say when"); Oryn's crossbow is with Lira with ONE bolt left. Brom's shoulder opened, was mended a second time and is BOUND, not mended a third ("It will not hold three times this season"). Cael's three old ribs took Compression again (ten days; quiet on the 129th).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:819:- **The brass on the hide (flag).** The Ternhall strip is slid down along itself with a pencil dot (a ruler "as long as its owner is patient"). Laid through the Fallow's ring and the Stair's, it runs into the carter's water-strokes and passes his third blank **a finger outside** Karis's pencilled ring. She moves nothing on the 115th. On the 120th she rubs the ring out and draws it where the cord put it, with *moved; paced.* On the 123rd she adds a fourth ring "at the very edge of the skin", with no word beside it; by the 131st a fine pencil line is ruled through the three centres and the fourth ring and off the hide. **What a ruler on a map of days is worth:** "a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm… a strip of country a mile wide." On the ridge she carries the line by ranging: Seln a furlong ahead, the last resting-place behind, moved until the three lie in a row. No compass direction is claimed for the line as a whole (ruled KEEP).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:821:- **The road to the Hall (flag):** the Stair road beyond the low ground, south and east under the ridge, for a day; then a grown-over track with a tree in one rut, for a day; then no track for some hours; then the track's last furlong straight down to the reeds. A mild spell on the 116th–118th; hard frost again from the night of the 120th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:835:- **Teague, days 115–121 (flag):** **he did not set the weir's third stake; the ford-keeper did not mark the shingle; the four marks were lost, as Movement 7 closed it.** He sent word to Pike by the mill's carter ("five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay"); followed the trail from the Stair for three days "for what's behind them, if there's anything", walking back a mile along it at each dusk; found nobody on it. He hears Karis's sentence and Cael's ruling on the 121st, watches the brass run off the hide, and asks nothing. His five reach Lowmarch before the five do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:839:- **Pike's first snow (flag; ruled KEEP):** "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw." He has done forty that day. The ninth line is given in narration (a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting; taken; six gone in; instruments found, brought up and kept by the crew that found them; six come out). The clerk "has been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to", and is given nothing. Pike turns the ledger toward Cael for the first time in this edition.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:851:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven** (the ford 1; the Ring 12; Oryn on the ridge 1; the Stair 9; the Hall 3; Oryn at the marsh 1). The trial point at the Hall's kerb on the 118th is logged and not counted. Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor: looked three times; unbound once (the Stair); a point set twice, of which one is the uncounted trial and one held the breach. Tide: Oryn twice, each time by her leave; himself three times (102, 119, 120); nobody else; never inside a line. **The still place:** his old log label, now a location under the breastbone, read three times; nothing moves in it; all ten go round it and none through; they do not pile against it; not the Quiet; not a fragment; told to Oryn at once and in order on the 120th, and to the other four in three sentences on the 121st; he has been told to write it down and stop touching it, and has not touched it since. Body: ribs quiet since the morning of the 129th; right forearm and left palm no longer remarked; calf a closed seam. Hesk's satchel: the log (the entries of the 118th, 119th, 120th, the fork, the crest, and the inventory of the 129th), the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and note, Ephram's and Vell's letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark, and Oryn's letter on bark. **He has Hesk's brass straightedge, and it reads true in his hand.** He has said "Noted" to Teague three times. He has not answered Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:853:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst in this movement. **Left arm slung high, all eight marks taken (read by Oryn at the fork on the 122nd), still under the order: it holds nothing until Oryn says, and she has not said; five weeks on the 131st.** It took no part in her two shots in the Hall, and it held nothing in a fall on the ice on the 127th. **Right ankle taped, never mended**; she walks on the whole sole; Oryn left her a roll at the fork. **She carries Oryn's crossbow on its strap, spanned, with one bolt in it**; she cannot span it; she shot it twice in the Hall, one-handed, and hit twice. She has not asked for the ankle again. She has not crossed, touched or tried the fourth line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:863:**Teague.** Unread and unasked. **Four marks poorer for the weir slip, which he forfeited.** He knows: six went into the Fallow and came out; one walked out of the Stair declaring more than one thing; six came out of the drowned hall wet; a strip of brass runs through three places on a hide and off its edge. He has asked "What are you." once and been answered "I don't have one." He knows nothing of the mechanism or of anything working inside a line. His term: "After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." He has given the five the forty-mark fence and has the cold road. His crew is unnamed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:865:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Nine lines** under the five names; the ninth is the Stair, with *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The season line said and written. **No number against it.** **Eleventh on the cloth, unchanged.** He has turned the ledger toward Cael once. The Fallow hundred and the Stair's three hundred lie in his box unmarked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:867:**Objects and money.** **Ninety-six marks in hand** (a hundred and two, less six to the inn). The reserve sealed in the third box, unopened. Three boxes against the warm wall of the back room. **The kit on the table**: the case in tarred canvas, the cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, the mallet; the ledger beside it. Two brass straightedges. The brown mule (the text does not say where she is stabled). Oryn's crossbow, hook and one bolt; four bolts are in the wold-wyrm. Owed: sixty yards of four-strand rope to the ferryman, "not this side of the thaw"; a reply to Ephram. The fence at forty is taken and not yet begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:869:**The Quieting, as known.** Four sites on one edge of brass. The Fallow Ring (stake 31), the Long Stair (32, on two bars, one with the stroke and the bar; the same mark on the kit's unused stake) and the Drowned Hall (33, old, no second) entered. The fourth seen and paced from outside on the 123rd: twice the radius by the bend of its cordon; stakes numbered 34 with the stroke and the bar, a pace apart; old ruts kept a hundred and ninety paces off; a standing grey shape that nobody could make out. **Not entered, not named, not dated, and not tried: no Arbiter has been taken to that line and what it does is unknown.** At the other three the live Arbiters stop at the line and Cael's fragments answer inside; whether a line twice the size does anything more is Lira's question and is unanswered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:871:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. The still place: the six. That fragments answer inside a line: the six. The Compact's side: a completion report on the Fallow; what the rim saw at the Stair; and now a clerk's packet saying that a crew kept a kit. Nobody outside the six knows that anything works inside. Nobody has said why. Nobody has put a verb between a made circle, an age with no ruler, four on a line, and a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:887:- **Lira:** left arm slung high, eight marks taken, holding nothing until Oryn says; right ankle taped and never mended; Oryn's spanned crossbow on its strap with one bolt, and Lira cannot span it (`manuscript/chapter-58.md:17-27`; `manuscript/chapter-59.md:25-37`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:231-237`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:889:- **Karis/research:** Ember, Iron Rank Three; voice whole; 52 sheets split 11/29/12; forty-one kinds at the Ring/Stair and thirty-three seen on the sampled Hall wall; four copies of the three-site leaf; carter's map with four unlabeled/aligned circles; Ternhall straightedge; kit field ledger; hypothesis unspoken and unentered (`manuscript/chapter-57.md:3-87`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:17-27,239-255`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:892:- **Board/work:** five names remain eleventh; nine completed lines; no number on the first-snow season line; Stair price says “three hundred; taken; not paid”; Teague's exact term is “After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes.”; the five take the forty-mark fence, whose day's posts and far side remain to do (`manuscript/chapter-59.md:167-247`). Teague's four-mark weir slip remains forfeited after fix 8.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:893:- **Money/accounts:** ninety-six living-purse marks (102 less six); sealed cart-and-gelding reserve still unopened in the third box; Fallow hundred and Stair three hundred remain in Pike's box and are not crew-payable debts; ferryman is owed sixty yards of four-strand rope, not wanted before thaw; fence not begun (`manuscript/chapter-52.md:357`; `manuscript/chapter-59.md:145-157,175-179,241-247`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:7-11,31`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:161:He had the ledger open already at their heading. He turned his shoulder to the room and gave out a line that nobody had yet heard, in the voice that he used under his eave. It was a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting. It was taken. Six had gone in. There were instruments found there, and brought up, and kept by the crew that found them; and six had come out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:187:"*Crew of six. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:When he came down he had a crossbow in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:185:Cael had known him for two years. He had seen him carve a goat, and wax a seal, and stand in a doorway with his back to a room, and he had never seen him do a thing in anger. Seln lifted the crossbow over his head and brought it down across the grey stone with the whole of his narrow strength, and the stock broke in two at the lock. He stood over it, breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:193:*Hundred and fourteenth day, night. Outside the stone. All six out. Brom: a bolt through the right shoulder, drawn and mended, an hour. Lira: the arm opened on the stair wall and is shut again, a quarter. Thirteen bolts loosed at us in the day, and one hit. Ours: five, and none shot.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:233:He was of the middle height, and heavy in the forearm, and there was a band of black leather laced on his right wrist. He stopped at the edge of their lamplight, and Cael saw his eyes go round it once, slowly. They went to a big man lying on his left side under a healer's coat, and to a girl whose sling was tied a hand higher than it had been at a certain door. They went to two pieces of a crossbow lying side by side by a grey stone. They came to Cael, and to his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:307:They were at the weir by first light on the hundred and fourteenth, and the woman who kept the ford told him the news before he had asked for it. Five had come off the ridge the day before, at noon, with a mule; and the healer had gone up again with them, and had taken the mill's jenny, and had left word with a carter for a child at some gate to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:351:That was a crossbow. He had expected a bow of some kind. He had not expected that every sign of its owner would stop at the height of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:He sent the spear south-about by the ruts with the lantern, and the boy with him. They were gone the better part of two hours. They came back by the north, having walked the whole of the ruts, with a crossbow each and a third across the boy's shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:373:It made Teague uneasy, and he did not pretend otherwise. But six people had lain on this rim with their faces covered and their bows unmarked, and had shot a man from behind; and one boy had stood up in front of them with a light held where everybody could see it. He knew which of those two things he would sooner have on a road beside his three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:167:He had seen four such stakes. There had been one at a grey stone on the Fallow, and two at the Stair with a road between, and one at the track's end beside a marsh. Each had stood alone at an edge where a cord had been laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:213:"I have not a single sheet of this place," said Karis. "I should like that entered beside whatever is ruled. I want it as I wanted the Stair. I am not going to say that I would go alone, since I have learned what that sentence costs other people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:15:Cael had lain against a bar of iron the night before, shaking, and had watched a lantern go away round the ruts to look for whatever six people had left; he had thought then that it was more than a captain owed to a crew ten lines under him, and he had not known how to say so. He did not know now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:She had said at the stone that she would not sit outside a second day and listen. Four people who knew what a crossbow was were holding the rim, and she meant to be where a certain shoulder was; if anything opened, it could be carried up forty steps and two hundred yards with her walking beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:He had been over the floor, and through the grass below the stone, and he had an armful of ash shafts, which he laid on the turf in a row beside four crossbows, one of which was in two pieces. Lira drew the one from her belt and laid it at the end of the row. There was an iron head lying by the stone that had no shaft to it; the boy looked at that, and at Brom, and went rather red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359:*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:205:The country kept one rule for fire, and voices, and Paths, all three together, and kept it only on a road; and that was the rule of people who knew that one of the three would kill them and had never been able to afford to learn which. A grey thing had walked a mile beside six people and done nothing, having been given nothing. It had heard a buckle, and had only followed. It had gone round a hundred feet of wet stones sooner than set a foot on them, and seven of its kind had drunk from a river today as if the river would burn them. A man had lifted with his whole Path for a long while, to the shoulders in that water, and been let alone; he had stood up on dry gravel with it still in his arms, and had not been. And a hound with its teeth in a silent man's hand had opened its mouth and left him for one who shouted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:207:"There's the ferry as well," she said. "I entered it on the fourteenth day and didn't know what I had. A boy shouted on that river as loudly as he pleased, and nobody on the boat so much as turned round. A river isn't a road, and I think the people out here know it in their feet and have never said why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:247:It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:261:Cael sat on in the loft door until the cold drove him in. Tomorrow there would be an east gate, and a road with a bend in it; and for the first time since he had crossed the Line, six people would go out by a gate together where there had always been five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:3:A hawk over that place at first light on the hundred and fourteenth day would have seen three rings, one inside another, and a line drawn across them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:There were two on the brow to the east, where the old road came over. One had a crossbow, with its nose on the ground and his boot in the stirrup; he was drawing the string with a hook, steadily, like a man doing a thing that he had done ten thousand times. The other stood two yards south of him and had nothing in his hands at all, and his arms were coming down to his sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:165:The six had not moved from their stations. On the brow a man was setting his boot in a stirrup for the third time. Nobody was running down the slope, and nobody was shouting. They had watched six people go into a hole in the ground, and it seemed that this was a thing that they had expected to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:189:The tinder caught. Seln held it to the lamp, and the small brass thing took the flame and steadied. Nobody had spoken since the fortieth step, and the only sound in that place was six people getting their breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:221:He got up, which he seldom did once he was down, and turned his shoulder to what was left of the room. He gave it out as he gave out a line: the recovery, and the Stair road, and the ridge, and the three hundred. He said that it was taken, and by whom. And then he said the other word, and he said it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:235:"You're taking the Stair contract."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:7:It was done as at the Stair, with one difference: nobody needed a sigil to find the way. Lira went along the reeds with the cord's end shut in her right fist and her boot half over the line, saying *gone* and *back* under her breath, and at every station her toe was within a hand of the first stem. Seln cut the turfs, which came up black and dripping. Cael wrote. His eyes had come back to him in the night, and he set down sixty-two stations in a better hand than the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:Karis went down her page with the pencil when Lira's fist came back to the grey stone. She turned to the leaf of the Stair and to the leaf of the Fallow, and wrote one line under the two that were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:139:Teague stood on that rise with his thumbs in his belt, and added it up. They had gone into the Fallow, six of them, and come out; a one-armed man had said so twice under his eave. They had gone into the Stair and one had walked out of it declaring. And here was the drowned hall, which nobody went near, and a rope of wet breeches, and six at a fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:51:"Then he found what I found, at the Stair. I should like it entered beside mine that he did, and by what."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:67:She did not trust that, and she set about breaking it. A straight thing will lie through any two marks on a skin, and so two of those dots proved nothing whatever; the whole question was the third. She moved the brass. She laid it through the Fallow's dot and the hall's, and looked at the Stair's: it sat under the edge. She laid it through the Stair's and the hall's, and the Fallow's sat under the edge. She took her pencil and made each dot again a pencil's breadth to one side, which was as far out as she believed that she could be on a map of days, and laid the brass a fourth time, and a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:163:It was what six people sounded like on a hard road. He knew the sound as he knew his own breathing, and he knew that he would learn it better tomorrow, when it was shorter by a pair of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:167:It came toward them a little after noon, behind a single mule, with a load of raw hides roped under a cloth and a smell going before it. The carter walked at the near wheel with a scarf over his nose. He looked at six people, and two beasts, and a sling, and an arm bound to a chest. He lifted one hand to them, as carters did on that side of the Line, and they lifted theirs, and nobody broke step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:The spark took on the third stroke. Karis's small brass lantern had come out of the middle box, and when its flame stood up Cael saw the camp that six people had made without meaning to. They were ten yards west of the grey stone, on the old line of the road, with the mule standing over them and her ears laid back. A long grey thing lay between them and the stone, where it had come down out of the air, and nobody looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:123:There were six of Thornwater's blankets on that mule, and there were six people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:7:He went over the ground once in his head, since six people were about to walk it with the light going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he left it for whoever kept findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:51:She had held it in since the fourteenth day, and Cael supposed that five weeks was a fair trial of anybody. Pike did not seem to mind, and went on counting silver for the woman with the red cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:215:He looked. A little way off in the stems there was a place where they leaned apart and closed again, and the place was moving. Nothing showed above the water. It went along beside the path as a pike goes along beside a boat, at exactly the pace of six people walking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:391:*In at first light. Karis hasn't said if since the Stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:"I have thought about that too," said Seln. "I could walk up those steps now, with this held over my head. They would take it. I do not think that the fence would come down afterward. A paper that sends six people to a hole in the ground is not concerned with what becomes of anybody who could say how the thing was collected." He paused. "I may be wrong. I have been inside that service, and I do not think that I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:231:It took the whole of his attention. He was a boy walking across thirty yards of bare stone in the last of the light, with six people looking at that stone; and he could not raise his eyes to them. He had them in his head only: two on the brow in front of him, two away on his left, one on his right, and one behind, where the west was still green. He walked with his eyes in the ground, and stepped over four bolts, and went round a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:281:It was on the far side of the iron, the side toward the brow, a hand above the turf, and he saw it through the bar, as a person sees a lamp through his own fingers. It was a single point, as big as a fist. Three courses came into it from the south, across the road; and three came into it from the north, round the whole of that great circle, the other end of the same rope. Nothing came out. Everything that held six people in a low place on the hundred and fourteenth day was hauled taut through that one point and belayed there, like the whole of a ship's rigging brought down to a single pin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:235:"So you'll all hear this, and you'll hear it once," said Oryn. "I'm coming in with you. I said I'd see him whole to the ninety-ninth, and I can't do that from a stone. But understand what you've got. If one of you puts a leg through something in there, I can pull it straight. I can splint it, and I can carry my end of you. I can't mend it. Not a scratch. Not till you're this side of that." She pointed at the stone with her chin. "Whatever happens in there happens to six people with no healer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:247:He read it over. Then he looked at six people on a bare ridge, and at the left-hand ruts, which ran on to the south with the white sun over them.
