editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:337:Every one of their chairs had a brass plate on its back with a name cut into it. He had read the plates from the floor with a borrowed glass on the first day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:341:He had the borrowed glass again now, and he put it to his eye. The chair was the same as the others, dark wood with a cane seat, and the registry's grey rail-cloth ran on in front of it without a break. There was a clean pale square on its back where a plate would have been screwed, and the screw holes were there, empty, from some earlier sitting. Somebody had sat in it once, at some other sitting, under some other plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:343:He looked at it for a long time. A plate, a steward and a brushed seat were all a chair could tell anybody from a public gallery, and this one had told him two of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:347:*Compact row, seat twelve. Held, unplated, empty, and guarded. Three times now a steward has cleared it, the last time of a man with a pie.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:349:*Every other chair in that row has a brass plate on its back. Twelve has a pale square where a plate used to be, two empty screw holes, a brushed seat, and a steward at the head of the stair. No name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:85:Rooke did not wait for the plates to be cleared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:107:Lira looked down at her plate, which was full and which she had not touched. Then, slowly, she picked up her fork and began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:271:Then her eyes went on down, past the quarterfinal, to the line she had worked out on the back of her hand on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:Cael had never seen her away from the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:21:On the hill she was the house, the way the long wall was the house, or the bell; she moved through it at its own pace and was part of its furniture. Here she seemed smaller and quicker, and a good deal younger. She laughed once at something Ephram said, a short surprised laugh that kept Ephram's eyes on his plate for the rest of the meal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:213:"She shortened the count," said Ephram. He was still breathing hard, and his face was red, but his voice was perfectly steady. "I found her wheel in one exchange, and she changed it in the next. Fifteen years of doing it one way, and she changed it in a breath because a boy from the bluff had found it." He looked down at his hands, half unwrapped. "I couldn't hit a pane before it was there. Nobody could. Not with an edge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:55:"I know you didn't. I said it to me." She shut the transcription and put it back on the shelf. "I keep deciding it, every time I go past. It's very tiring. The season first. Then the other thing." She came back to the map and picked up another pin. "It isn't going anywhere. It's been in that book longer than the house has been on this bluff."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:57:Below the window, at the foot of the bluff road, two coats stood in the snow in the lee of the wall, and two more stood at the ferry landing on the far side of the river, small and dark against the white, where Cael could see them from here as he could see them from every high window on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:The block's inventory went into the Log that night, with the ferry landing white and empty below except for its two coats. Most of it said what it always said, so he wrote those lines short: the oak's six free a day and the landing beat no shorter; the right shoulder unasked for anything since the mill town's plate; Compression shown nowhere, not even for the barge-master's fall; Shadow's rent from waking, and one drift on the stair. Then he wrote the two lines that were new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:129:He had written the roster himself that afternoon, in fair copy, for posting on the board in the covered walk and for the regional office and for Norhold. Eleven names. Five competitors, the coach and the evaluator of record, two reserves, a wagoner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:135:He signed the report, sealed it with the office's plain seal, and put it in the pouch for the north. The pouch would go down the bluff on the morning coach, past the two coats at the foot of the road. They would not look at it. Nobody ever looked at the pouch; that was what a pouch was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:169:The snow had stopped. The sky had cleared and was full of stars, and the river below was loud and dark and fast, with ice going down it in plates that knocked together in the current with a sound like somebody far off moving furniture.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:Cael woke to the sound of water. It was running everywhere, off the roofs and down the gutters and along the covered walk, and in the morning the snow on the bluff had gone grey and soft and was sliding off the tiles in long wet sheets. The river under the wall had risen a man's height overnight and ran brown and heavy and very fast, carrying whole trees down past Ostrand toward the confluence, and the bluff road was a stream for a day, and then deep mud, and then, on the morning of the Second-day, a road again, or near enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:213:The wagons went out through the gate and down the bluff road in the thin early light, with the mud sucking at the wheels and the whole valley below them steaming in the first sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:Nobody had arranged that either. It was market day, and the town was out, and somebody had seen the wagons coming down the bluff. By the time they reached the bridge there were perhaps two hundred people standing along it in the mud, with more coming down the lanes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:There were the baker's boys and the ferry crew and the women from the fish steps, and the man who sold hot chestnuts at the bottom of the hill. There were children on their fathers' shoulders. A great many of them had never set foot on the bluff in their lives.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:235:Above it the bluff stood up dark against the morning, with the house on top of it, and the wall where Lira had sat a few nights before, looking north, in the starlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:The watchers grew smaller behind them, two and two, standing in the melt on the bell, and then the road bent and the bluff went behind a line of willows, and they were gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:At supper Lira ate with her left hand flat on the table beside her plate, and every so often she glanced down at it. Ephram bore this for as long as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:21:Brom had not said anything. He had a folded paper of his own beside his plate, and he waited until the others had finished with the broadsides before he unfolded it, because Brom read things at the table or not at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:207:There was exactly one name above, and every one of them had been not saying it since the tunnel. It had become a kind of rule among them without anybody making it, the way the long table's rules got made: the name was not to be spent before its time. Brom ate his supper. Ephram looked at his plate. Lira, who left nothing unsaid that she wanted said, put her cup down and looked across at Cael and said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:He had read it before. He had read it at Ardenmere, before it was a file, when it was a form with four questions on it and a boy on a pump step. He had read it at Greyvane across the end of a hearing table, and on the bluff with the rain coming down on the canvas. Now it had a season grown onto it: four meets, thirteen exhibitions, a column of figures, a house first in its region. It was thicker than any file on the boy had ever been, and in the administrative layer underneath it, where nobody but a records officer ever looked, the same mark stood where it had always stood, as plain and as orphaned as on the day he had first found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:He did not write this one down. There was no fifth entry. The rule of the notebook was that it held what had happened and nothing of what he made of it. He had broken that rule once, on the bluff, and did not mean to make a habit of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:The Archmarshal Vastin read the Norhold files on a Fourth-day evening, in his room in the Compact's house, a week before the opening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:It was a plain document, a plan of twelve seats drawn in a clerk's neat hand, with a name-plate entered against each. The six observation seats came first, then the district officers, then a liaison seat, then two left for officials of the host city. He read along them in order. One, two, three. Eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:231:There was no plate entered against it. Where the name should have been there was a short instruction in the same clerk's hand. *Seat twelve: held. No plate. Stewards to keep the seat clear for the length of the sitting.* Beneath that, in the column for the grade of the officer for whom a seat was held, the clerk had written one word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:235:Vastin sat back in his chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:The Archmarshal Vastin had reached the city the evening before, by the post road, with one bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:He followed her along the back of the gallery, past the public benches and behind the row's plated chairs, to the end, where the grey rail-cloth ran on in front of a chair with a pale square on its back and two empty screw holes. She lifted the cloth for him and let it fall again when he had sat down, and went back to her stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:39:There was no limp, quite. There was a boy walking as a man walks across a room he knows has a loose board in it, keeping his weight on one side and not letting anybody see him keep it. Vastin had seen a great many people walk like that into a great many rooms, and very few of them had been sixteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:41:The whole bowl watched the showman from the lake house, and there was nothing to learn from looking where eight thousand people were already looking. Vastin watched the boy, as he had once sat four feet from him in a room twelve feet by fourteen and watched him while he answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:43:What he saw in the second exchange he had never seen done on a floor. The boy declined to be told a story. Each of the showman's passages leaned toward its ending, and the bowl leaned with it, and at the moment before the ending the boy was quietly somewhere else, at no speed at all, until the passage had nothing left to land on. It was all in the open, inside the rule, in front of everyone, and as far as Vastin could tell not one person in the building was looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:51:In the third exchange the boy took a touch standing in the light, and in the instant before it Vastin saw something cross his face that he could not read. It was not surprise and it was not fear. It was very quick and then gone, and he did not write it down, because a thing he did not know could not go into a book as though he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:75:He went along the back of the row without hurrying, behind the plated chairs, past Ilsev, who did not turn, and past Havel, who did. He nodded to Havel. He went down the east stair, where the young steward stood aside for him with her list against her chest, and out by the side door into the street, where the afternoon was going gold over the roofs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:83:Supper was half eaten before anybody spoke of Vastin, and it was Cael who did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:The table took that in. Nobody asked how he knew it was Vastin; he had described that stillness to them often enough over the year, and Karis had already had the rest of it from Lira, who had watched the twelfth chair through the whole bout from the north rail instead of watching the floor, and had told everybody afterward, with some indignation, that she had missed the best touch of the afternoon on Cael's account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:The likeness-seller took the copper and made change and handed him the sheet rolled up, and said "Good luck to the bluff," because the sheet had the bluff's name on it, and did not look at his face at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:29:Through the nearest window he could see the long table at the back, and the five of them round it, and the plates. He could also see, along the side of the room, the counter, where a man could sit on a stool with a cup in front of him and his back to the wall and see the whole room and everybody in it, and not be at anybody's table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:Brom was opposite him, with his wrapped forearms resting on the table and a plate of something in front of him that had certainly not been there when the proprietor opened her door. As Cael sat, a girl from the kitchen came and put down a basket of bread between them. Then she brought a pot of mussels in a dark, buttery broth, and a dish of potatoes cut thick and salted with grey salt, and went away again. Nobody had asked her for any of it. Brom looked at the mussels with mild interest, as a man looks at weather he had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:77:She had them under her on the bench, palms down, both of them, and when she saw him see it she went faintly pink and took them out and put them on the table, and they lay there, empty. There was no notebook beside her plate. There was no document wallet at her feet or pencil behind her ear. She had come to the harbour with nothing whatever to write on, and she had found, by the look of her, that her hands did not know what to do about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:93:"It's balanced," he said, sitting down, still breathless. "The whole gate. It weighs as much as this building and it's balanced so exactly that one man can move it with one hand, if he moves it at the right time. If he moves it at the wrong time, nothing on earth can shift it." He unfolded the paper and laid it among the plates. "He gave me this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:He crossed the shop to the long table. He put the parcel down in front of Cael, and laid a card and an envelope on top of it, and said, "The pouch. This morning's," in exactly the voice he used across the counter at home on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:Brom saw it too. Without looking up from his plate he put out one wrapped hand and moved the pot of mussels six inches along the table, to the empty end of the bench beside him, where nobody was sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:121:Then it went on, and five people managed it without a word between them. Lira, in the middle of telling Ephram that he would not be allowed to wind anything else today, reached down the table without looking and set a clean cup in front of Seln and filled it from the cider jug, and went on talking. Brom went back to his plate. Ephram turned round and asked Seln, quite seriously, whether the office happened to know what the harbour's lock gates were made of, because the lock-keeper had said oak and Ephram thought he had seen iron, and Seln said, "Both," and Ephram said, "I knew it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:129:Cael ate his fish. He kept his face on his plate. He put the moment away very carefully, in the place where he kept the few things he meant to have for the rest of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:193:It was a brown honey-cake of the northern kind, sticky on top and plain underneath. Lira borrowed a knife from the next table and cut it into six pieces, and looked at them, and moved one by a hair, and served them round. Brom did not look up from his plate. Lira looked at him down the whole length of the table while she did it, steadily, until Karis laughed out loud at the two of them, which happened about once a season.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:243:"The office has signed for a good many parcels since the bluff," he said. "Kit. Floor declarations. Two crates of the chancellor's papers and one of Gault's instruments, which he would not let out of his sight on the coach. The office does not as a rule mind handing parcels over." He looked at the book on the table in front of Cael, and then, for a moment, at Cael. "That is the first one it has been sorry to hand over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:11:Cael stood at the west gate with the others and listened to the roar and read it, because reading things was what he did when he was frightened. Under it he could hear two hopes at once, the holders' and the bluff's, shouted by the same people in the same breath. The holders against the house with the blank. The Gold against the boy nobody could rate. He did not write any of it down. There would be no time for writing on that floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:133:Two exchanges. The marshals had chalked them both up beside the bluff's name, in a sanctioned book, in front of everybody who mattered in the business of fighting, and no clerk alive could rub them out again. It had been counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:Nobody ever had. In a year of wardens and evaluators and brass plates and panels, everybody who looked at him had been looking for a line to fill in or a figure to post. Nothing in her face was going to be written down anywhere. She meant to find out what he was before she went on, because he was on none of her tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:21:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Flat on two plates on the bluff and one in a felt bag at the mill town. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder. Not asked for since the mill town's plate.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:71:"No price on the fifth man of the bluff! None! The house won't quote him! Come and see the thing we can't price!"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:207:"Within the hour of opening," said Bracken. "I'm told the Chief Adjudicator read the rule aloud once and approved it without a hearing." He sat down, which he rarely did at their table, and took a piece of bread, which he more rarely did. "I'm also told he was relieved. The trial office wants it known that it was not relieved, being an office, but that he was." He buttered the bread with great precision. "I don't mind telling you I was relieved myself. I've been sleeping badly on that clause for a year. Last night I slept like a quarry stone." He considered. "That's twice since the bluff. I may be getting used to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:155:The Archmarshal Vastin had come to Norhold, and he was sitting in the chair that had been held for two weeks for somebody nobody would name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:*The Archmarshal Vastin asks the enrollee for one hour, before the houses leave the city. Nothing will be written by either party. It would be better if nothing were brought to write with. The timekeepers' office in the east tower has lent a room for the purpose; it belongs to neither the adjudication office nor the Compact. He is free to say no; it will count against him nowhere, with no one, at any time; and that sentence is not a formality.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:"Nothing written," she said. "Think about what that means for us. This whole house runs on one rule, and it's kept you alive since the bluff. If it's on paper, we can survive it. Every word of the hearing was written down, and that's why we could fight it. The provision's on paper. The roster was on paper and that's the only reason you were on a floor at all." She touched the note with one finger. "He's asking you to walk out of the one place we've ever been safe. Whatever's said up there, afterwards there's his memory of it and yours, and his comes with a grey coat and a seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:"I asked for a witness once," he said, "with a warden across a table from me. I was right to. That man was a piece of the machine, and you don't sit down with a piece of the machine without somebody to say afterward what was said." He looked at the note. "This one's different, and I've had a year to learn how. Eleven questions in a room on the bluff. Three sentences of advisory. A chair held for him that he never asked for, two notes, and a walk out before the figure." He turned the note over. "Every move he's ever made toward me he's made in ink, and signed with his whole name. This is the first thing he's ever asked for that he can't sign. A man like that doesn't give up his ink to set a trap. He'd use the ink." He folded it. "I want to know what's worth that much to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Vastin was sitting at the long table with his back to the window. He got up when Cael came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:145:"Thank you for coming," said Vastin, and sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:153:Vastin looked round the room before he did, at the shelves of clocks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:163:"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"The first four are words you use about a fault," said Vastin. "A thing gone wrong in the record, which a careful office would like to find and mend. You find a fault so that you can take it out." He moved his left hand an inch on the wood. "The second four are words you use about a thing you mean to keep in a box. I went through the Compact's charter for them, article by article, one evening. They aren't in it. You can't write *handling* about a question. You can only write it about an answer you've already got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:183:Then Vastin lifted his eyes to Cael's and kept them there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:209:Vastin did not answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:213:"I won't answer that one," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:219:"No." Vastin did not soften it. "That is the other half of why I asked for you. What you do now makes no difference to it. A year ago it would have. A thing you do is an answer, and answers are for questions, and they have stopped asking. Nothing answers a conclusion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:Vastin did not ask what he meant. He looked, for a moment, very slightly pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:135:"I have," she said. "It's called having a house." She put her good hand on his arm for a moment. "Go and eat something. Brom's ordered for you. He hasn't told anybody, but there's a plate in the common room with your name on it in gravy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:11:Brom walked out across the grey stone to the table. His forearms were still wound in linen from wrist to elbow, and he carried them a little out from his sides. The clerk lifted the plaque off the grey cloth and gave it to him. It was a plain board of dark wood with a brass plate let into it, the tournament's bar-and-ring cut at the top and a line of small type underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:17:Cael had seen Lira hold the Copper Crown like that, once, on the bluff. He had not expected ever to see it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:He carried it up the guesting-house stair the same way and set it on the long table beside his plate, upright, against the water jug, where it stayed all through supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:49:When he came back he had the envelope folded small in his fist. He went to his kit against the wall, opened it, and put the envelope in at the very bottom, beside nothing else Cael had ever seen him keep there. Then he came back and sat down in front of his plate and the plaque.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:71:Withrow came up the stair late that same evening, when the plates had been cleared, which she seldom did. She sat down at the end of the long table with a cup of the guesting-house's poor wine and listened to Brom's plaque being discussed for a while, and said nothing until it had been discussed enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:95:Nobody said a word about it, then or at any board between that one and the bluff, and nobody ever would; but every night, at every board, every one of them counted him. They did it the way you count heads at the door at the end of a long day, to be sure that everybody you care about has come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:157:On the eleventh day, at a waystation where the coast road came in from the south, the coach from the coast pulled into the yard with its horses steaming while they were eating, and stood there blowing while the coachman got down. The coachman put his head in at the door and asked if there was anybody here from the bluff, and held up a letter. Cael knew the hand from across the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:169:There were two of them, at the far end of a bridge in a market town, standing by a hired horse where the road came off the bridge into the square. Grey coats and good boots. Cael knew both faces. He had counted them for two years from the bluff's walls and windows and the ferry steps. He counted them now from the wagon's bench, and was surprised to find that he was very nearly glad of them. After a city of eight thousand who had all been looking at him at once, two men and a horse were almost homely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:173:*Two of them, at a bridge. The old arrangement, two and a horse. After Norhold it looks small, and I find I'm fond of it. But Vastin spent an hour telling me the old way is finishing. So: noted, and kept beside his hour. They may be the last watchers I get whose names I could learn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:181:Ephram spent the last three days working out how to tell it on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:185:"We went down there and they counted us, and the count's on a staff in the middle of the continent where anyone can read it. And one of us they couldn't count." He considered it. "There's a longer version. It comes with my kit, up the bluff, carried by whoever asked for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:First the river, wide and slow and going copper in the late light; then the roofs of Ostrand, slate and brown, coming up out of the valley along it; and last, above them all, the bluff, with its one long roofline holding the sun after everything below had lost it. The wagons went up the climb with nobody saying much, because it was the last mile of a long year, and every one of them had found that it was enough just to be riding it, together, with the light going off the water behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:It came along the curve of the barrier from the south-east, close under the cap, and it did not run straight. It followed the wall round. He had no word for it. Nobody on the bluff had a word for it, because Karis had drawn every lane on her wall as a straight line, and every lane he had ever seen had been one. This one curved like a river round a meadow, and he heard it curve, and only just got off the end of it before it reached him. He came out of it with his heart going and his back against the north mast's cable, and he thought, quite clearly, *She has never shown that to anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:11:"*Auremont went home with the bout. The bluff went home with the first thing anybody has taken off Auremont all week, and there is no column in the standings to put it in.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:17:Brom ate his breakfast in silence. He ate all of it, and then he ate the end of the loaf, which nobody contested, and then he sat with his hands on the table on either side of his plate and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:"I know. I've been thinking about the second half." He looked round at them. "Plan to have fewer. That's not plan to have none. You can't have none. Nobody can. Rooke knows that better than anybody." He stood up and began to clear his own plate, which he never did. "So the question isn't how many I've got. It's which ones he gets to keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:High on the east side, over the far rail, the Compact's row was full except for one chair. Ilsev sat in the first seat with her lap-desk on her knees. Havel was in the second. At the end of the row, behind the grey rail-cloth, the twelfth chair stood empty, with its brushed seat and its pale square where a plate should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:At the end of the row was the twelfth chair. It had been held, unplated, for thirteen days before anybody sat in it, and had been empty again since the day of the duelist. There was a man in it now. He wore the plain grey of an inspecting officer and nothing on his collar. Seln said he had come in by the east stair an hour before the brackets began, alone, with no case and no clerk, and had sat down, and had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:And as he read her he understood what she was doing. Her lanes were not full. He had seen her full lane on the trial floor and these were not that. They ran a little short, and a little shallow, and each one came at a slightly different speed from the one before, as if she were turning a dial. She was not trying to finish him. She was finding out where he stopped. It was what Gault did with a plate before he measured anything against it: take the baseline first, honestly, so that every reading after it would mean something. She was taking his. And then she would fight a little above it, so as not to waste either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:Vastin went down the tower stair at his ordinary pace and out under the arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:9:He had written a note like that himself, once, on the road home from the bluff. Fewer words, and in a worse light, and signed with his whole name. He had thought it careful at the time. He saw now that the west tower had done it better. They had not tried to say what the boy was. They had said only what their instrument could and could not do, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:"The minute from the bluff held," she said. "I want that said first, before anything else, because it's true. Nothing was made. She began it. You were awake and you knew what you were doing." She laid the sheet on the table. "And the third line held too, as far as it went. The cost was on paper. Every cost we had ever seen was on paper." She looked at him. "The trouble is we had never seen one at that weight. We had it written down at the weight of a stair, and a yard, and a copper pot. Not at the weight of a Gold, in the middle of a bout, in front of a city."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:115:*First honest warning I've ever gotten from inside the machine. He built no case, made no ask, took no note. He just told me the weather. The Compact's best evaluator thinks the next thing that comes for me won't wear a uniform or file a challenge. Write it down. Believe it. Keep competing anyway — but from tonight, we build like people who believe it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:139:The steward took the bluff's standard, which was plain and not new and had been mended once at the hoist by somebody's careful needle a long time ago, and carried it past the whole row of fourteen staffs to the head of the floor. He fixed it to the first of the three tall staffs there, the honour staffs. Then he ran it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:163:Cael thought it was exactly like the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:Inside, it did not break at all for a long while. From the north barrier Cael could see the dais, and nothing on it moved. Nine pens lay still on nine open charts. Behind them, at their own table, one of the three scribes had stopped and was looking at the end of his pen, as if it had done something without asking him, while the other two wrote as fast as their hands would go. High over the east rail, in the Compact's row, the quiet records officer from the bluff had stood up, and did not seem to know that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:230:At the north rail Rooke had taken his hand back. He said something to the air, but the younger healer heard it and said it again at her own supper table that night, and by the end of the week it had reached the bluff by three roads. It was the version the house kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:234:Cael looked up once more, past the officials, to the high row over the east rail. Every face in it was turned down toward the floor. Every face but one. The man in plain grey at the end of the row was standing, and going along behind the plated chairs toward the east stair, at the same unhurried pace he had used on the day of the duelist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:169:"And what it costs." He did not look away. "Every plate anybody's ever set in front of me read me against those rates. Every shop's file was written against them. They're why nobody's ever quite known what to ask of me. After tomorrow whatever I show is in the book, and nobody will ever again measure me against less. There's no getting it back into the box." He put both palms down on the table. "A rate you have shown is a rate you have shown forever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:243:*Quenna asked if I could not-do it in front of people who wish me harm. I learned to. It's under everything else I know how to do. It's held in front of wardens and panels and men with plates, and in front of a bowl of eight thousand people on their feet, and I never once had to wonder.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:69:The roar, when the five of them came out, was a roar for plain colours, and Cael could hear the difference. Somewhere in the last fortnight the port had decided they were its own. It had not asked them. A city did not ask. It had simply begun to say *the bluff* the way it said *the harbour*, and it had come out on a dull afternoon, with its bedsheets, to make sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:235:Cael stood in the line with the other four round him and did not move. Eighteen years the house on the bluff had been missing from the lists, and nobody who now taught there had ever stood on this floor in its colours. The whole year had just gone into the office's book in a stranger's voice, line by line, initialled, and there was no clerk anywhere who could reach in and take a line of it out again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:111:For a year the Archmarshal Vastin had kept the Compact's record on the enrollee of Halcenvane, and kept it honest, and a week before he had sat in a held chair at Norhold to watch the boy fight a duelist, and written twice, and gone home before the figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:125:For some weeks now it had been lighter. The bound digests that used to come down from the higher office under its seal, with no routing slip and no initials, had come at first every few days and then every week, each with its careful new words for old things and its polite request for his view on how a matter might be *framed*. He had answered every one as an evaluator answers, with what was true and nothing else. Then, somewhere in the middle of the sitting, they had stopped. He had sent his last answer up the stair and nothing had come down it since: no thanks, no question, not so much as an acknowledgement of receipt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:127:Vastin had been in the Compact's service long enough to know that silence came in sorts, and that an officer who could not tell one sort from another would spend his whole career being startled. There was the silence of a matter forgotten, which gathered dust and did no harm. There was the silence of a matter settled, which sat comfortably on a shelf. There was the silence of a matter waiting for somebody who was away, which had a fidget in it that could be felt through a shut door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:129:And there was a fourth sort, which he had met perhaps three times in all his years, and had not liked on any of them. It was the silence that falls when you have been taken off a list. Nobody above him had stopped caring about the boy. They had only stopped caring what Vastin thought. His evaluations were going up the stair as truthfully as ever, and nobody at the top of it was reading them, because whatever was going to be done was going to be done on other papers than his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:147:His clerk came back, and saw the empty week, and wrote the journey into it without a word. Vastin let him. When he had gone, Vastin sat on at the desk with the lamp not yet lit and the pigeons settling on the ledges of the court below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:167:*An evaluation that begins from its conclusion is a report about the evaluator. Somewhere above him, a report about the evaluators was being written, and he intended to see the evidence himself before it was.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:*Gold Rank 3. The system's favorite. And the loneliest read I've taken since Vastin — except hers is lonely like mine. Two days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:It gave his name, and his house on the bluff above some northern river, and the clause of an old charter that had obliged the house to enter him. It gave fifteen exhibition figures, with a note that he had lost none of the bouts behind them. It gave a circuit record from early on in his fighting, in a city called Ardenmere, and the circuit record had losses in it, which the shop printed without comment, as if it could not decide whether they mattered. It used the phrase *peculiar timing* four times. And it had a new last leaf, sold separately for a copper, which said in italics that he had survived her lane at trial speed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:She had been Auremont's since twelve, a year before the garden. The house had seen something in a set of entrance papers and sent for her, and kept her near, and waited for the rest. Afterwards nothing in her life had been left to happen by itself. Floors were relaid to her measure. An evaluator who wanted a look at her had his station opened before the day began, and came, and looked. Whole classes were assembled round her and broken up again when she grew past them. Other people spoke of climbing; she had never once had to look for the next rung. It was always there, put down where her foot would go, by people who were very good at their work. At Auremont the day of the garden court was a half-holiday now, with a demonstration in the afternoon.

