editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:231:Seln gave it a count of sixty a side. Cael heard him do it, under his breath, exactly; and when the cake came off the stone it was pale gold on both faces, and risen, and there was not a black place on it anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:255:He looked round the fire at five faces. Every one of them had watched a thing go north out of a lane that they had been certain could not happen, and had walked beside him for a day without a word about it. They had not gone quiet, or careful. The big man had let another person make his bread sooner than leave the others without, and the small one had made a joke of it on paper, and Lira had told a carter's boot what she thought of it. It was the same crew that had sat at a fire above a wash, with a healer added, who was looking at her own heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:273:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:351:She was not being short with him. It was only how a healer's table was run: nobody was promised what the hands would find; they were told afterward, whatever it was, to their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:237:Brom made bread that night, with the old man's flour, on a flat stone at the edge of their fire. He had seen it done, he said, and it could not be hard. The cakes came off the stone black on both faces and white in the middle, and Lira weighed one in her hand and asked whether the smith had been consulted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:55:Karis had drawn her notebook out; she looked round at fourteen faces, and laid it on her knee, shut, with one finger in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:47:*The roads were honest and the food wasn't.* He thought of a noon road that had told him the truth, and of two cairns set on a slant; and then he thought of Brom's bread, which had come off a hot stone black on both faces and white in the middle, and he laughed aloud, alone on a river bank, so that a heron got up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:227:He had the ruling. He was the nearest thing that they had to a captain, and it was his to say which way and how fast. That was the whole of what was in those five faces. Not one of them was asking him anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:109:"They'll send people. A lure's no use if nobody's waiting at the end of it. People have faces. Six years I've wanted a face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:291:*People have faces.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:91:The big man had got there as well. His eyes went over the faces, and the fires, and came back to the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:211:It was a corridor of air the length of a cart and team, high at his end and low at the other, and everything in it went downhill at once. The pale grass stood along both lips of the rut with its seed heavy on it. The lane stripped it. Seed and chaff and dry stem went down that channel in a flood, as thick as meal out of a sack, straight into the faces of whatever was coming up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:Oryn had loaded the mule in the hollow. Two of the boxes hung from the pack-saddle, one on either flank, and her own panniers were lashed on top with the crossbow in its cover. She took the animal by the cheek-strap and walked it to the edge of the break, and there she changed her hand and put herself on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:213:It was the second night down, and the first on which nobody was too cold or too sore to talk. Seln had the fire, and Brom had made a bread of the healer's flour on a hot stone; it had come off black on both faces, as his bread did, and everyone ate it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:97:He had been waiting for it without knowing that he waited. He had lain under hands before, and under eyes; he knew to the breath when it came. The thing reaching for him would find that he was not what it had been built to hold, and then it would change. A brass plate went dark. A panel's faces shut. A clerk with a form that had no box for him wrote a word in capitals, because he could find no tier to write; and after that there was a file, and a watcher at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:He went over it as he went over a load. Six people had lain round that low ground for a day with their faces covered, and had gone off it north and south and west when a boy stood up with a light. They had gone as people go who have practised leaving. He had read where they lay, by lantern, and had found nothing above the height of a boot. People who take such pains over what they leave behind are not people who go home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:131:The hold had eaten. Its fourteen had drawn off to the far end of that low room, to their mending and their dice and an old man asleep in the chimney corner, and they did not stare; but Cael felt himself counted from under a good many eyelids. The board had been scrubbed. Six bowls stood on it, and six horn spoons, and a round of bread that was black on both faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:73:There was the bread, which was his own, and black on both faces. He broke it into six by feel, and ate his share with a great deal of noise, so that the healer should hear him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:When he came down he had a crossbow in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:185:Cael had known him for two years. He had seen him carve a goat, and wax a seal, and stand in a doorway with his back to a room, and he had never seen him do a thing in anger. Seln lifted the crossbow over his head and brought it down across the grey stone with the whole of his narrow strength, and the stock broke in two at the lock. He stood over it, breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:187:"No faces."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:233:He was of the middle height, and heavy in the forearm, and there was a band of black leather laced on his right wrist. He stopped at the edge of their lamplight, and Cael saw his eyes go round it once, slowly. They went to a big man lying on his left side under a healer's coat, and to a girl whose sling was tied a hand higher than it had been at a certain door. They went to two pieces of a crossbow lying side by side by a grey stone. They came to Cael, and to his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:351:That was a crossbow. He had expected a bow of some kind. He had not expected that every sign of its owner would stop at the height of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:He sent the spear south-about by the ruts with the lantern, and the boy with him. They were gone the better part of two hours. They came back by the north, having walked the whole of the ruts, with a crossbow each and a third across the boy's shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:373:It made Teague uneasy, and he did not pretend otherwise. But six people had lain on this rim with their faces covered and their bows unmarked, and had shot a man from behind; and one boy had stood up in front of them with a light held where everybody could see it. He knew which of those two things he would sooner have on a road beside his three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:She had said at the stone that she would not sit outside a second day and listen. Four people who knew what a crossbow was were holding the rim, and she meant to be where a certain shoulder was; if anything opened, it could be carried up forty steps and two hundred yards with her walking beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:He had been over the floor, and through the grass below the stone, and he had an armful of ash shafts, which he laid on the turf in a row beside four crossbows, one of which was in two pieces. Lira drew the one from her belt and laid it at the end of the row. There was an iron head lying by the stone that had no shaft to it; the boy looked at that, and at Brom, and went rather red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:The first was the one that she had lit, at the track's end, with wet people round it and a line of breeches steaming on a rope between two bundles of reeds. The second she built for herself, twenty yards off and upwind, no bigger than a hat, since she would not have sparks near paper. She had the lid of the middle box for a table there, and her morning's work laid out on the turf under pebbles, one sheet beside another, to lose the damp that they had taken from a wall. She had gone into that place for the inner faces of four walls, and had come out with as much of one of them as a person can rub upside down in an hour with a girl holding her by the belt; and she set about it now as if the hour had been a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:67:Her right shoulder met the middle course exactly as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in the dark. She stopped as if she had been struck. Brom's weight came on past her, and his chest found it, and he made a sound that Cael did not wish to hear again; and the two of them stood swaying against nothing at all, a yard short of a grey stone, with the sun in their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:There were two on the brow to the east, where the old road came over. One had a crossbow, with its nose on the ground and his boot in the stirrup; he was drawing the string with a hook, steadily, like a man doing a thing that he had done ten thousand times. The other stood two yards south of him and had nothing in his hands at all, and his arms were coming down to his sides.
