editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:His room was high in the Compact's house, up a stair that the builders had made too steep and the clerks had worn too smooth. Its one window gave on a court where a man in a porter's apron fed pigeons every morning at the same hour from the same paper bag. Vastin had come to regard the pigeons and the porter as the most reliable instrument in the building. The room held a table, two chairs, and four shelves of files that were his only in the sense that he was the officer who would be asked about them. It was a little after the second bell. His left hand, which had begun some years ago to dislike mornings, lay on the table beside the bundle and declined to be hurried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:A registrar who had been answered against his memorandum would write within the week, wanting language to stand behind: a sentence he could quote back to the house so that the house would know it was the Compact and not the regional office that had given way. Or an office above would write, wanting to know why the Compact's evaluator had once again declined to hand it a difficulty. One or the other. Usually both. He had the answers to both in his drawer, beneath the struck page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:141:He held it. He went on refusing, on worse ground than before, with the racks at his shoulder and the ropes closing, and he paid for every refusal on the contested boards in a way he had not paid in the first two exchanges. The yard-master's steps came closer together. Twice Cael had to turn hard off his left foot to keep out of the old man's reach, and the second time something in the back of his left calf drew tight, a long thin pull from the knee to the heel, like a cord being wound. He felt it and noted it and kept moving. He did not burst. He did not touch. He gave the panel a third exchange in which a boy kept himself alive on two-thirds of a floor and did nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:291:In the fourth exchange Ephram threw the second-year entry three times, and each time he threw it differently. The first time he did not turn the front foot at all, and the Blade, who was waiting to turn with it, turned with nothing and had to recover. The second time he turned it early instead of late, before the heel. The third time he threw the plain entry, everything at once, no second weight, the one he had mixed in on the very first morning on the second floor at home to keep Cael honest. And the Blade, who had spent three exchanges and a whole bout at the wool town learning to read a two-weight entry with great patience, read the one-weight entry as a two-weight entry for a quarter of a beat too long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:127:Cael read the stack fast and then a second time slowly, and the room around him went quiet, as rooms did when he did that. The read was no use on paper; paper had no weight. But he had a second thing that worked on paper, which was the habit of years spent reading men who wanted to be misread, and it found him more in Rooke's three-word margins than in the long lines of record beside them. A man who wrote *studies you* about an opponent was paying her the highest compliment Rooke owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:277:That was all he said at the midday table about either bout, and he said it to the bread. He had his left forearm on the board beside his bowl, already darkening under the skin from wrist to elbow. He had declined the ice, the salve and Ephram's opinion, in that order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:153:"Go and train," she said. "If their charter holds, you've a season starting in a little over three weeks. If it doesn't, you've a quarrel starting rather sooner, and I'd like you fit for that too." She took her spectacles off, which in Withrow was the same as a door opening. "Thank Karis for me. Don't let her refuse it. Tell her it's from the chancellor and she has no standing to decline."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:69:At the trestle the five judges were writing, each alone, with their slates held close. He wondered what there was to write. *Execution*: clean feet, no waste. *Control*: a boy who never once let himself be put where he was being sent. *Effect*: nothing. He had done nothing whatever to the man but decline him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:223:He declined all six. He gave the panel what it needed to rate: clean feet, a read that looked like decent timing, two touches on the right lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The entry instrument lay open on the records-hall counter with its four companions stacked beside it, and Bracken had turned it round so that Cael could read it right way up, which he did not need. It was the fourth day of term, a Fifth-day, in the hour after the morning's session let out, and the stone hall was empty except for the three of them and the clerk at the far desk, who was copying something with his tongue between his teeth. Bracken had sent a boy to the residence with the first bell, carrying a half-sheet that said *Records hall, the hour after session. Bring nothing.* Nobody else had been asked. Withrow had declined to come down, and had said so to Bracken in Cael's hearing in the covered walk the afternoon before, in five words: "It's a filing. File it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:93:"Everyone's going to say we did something this morning. In a fortnight half the circuit will be telling the other half that Halcenvane played a trick at the registry, and they'll be very pleased with themselves for spotting it." She did not look up from the page. "We didn't put you on the roster. We just declined to commit fraud by leaving you off. The clause does the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:39:"At whoever can make him." Then she lifted her eyes from the sheet and found Cael's across the table, and held them. There was nothing unkind in it. It was the look she gave a document that had just told her something she had already suspected. "Two bouts, a sheet of figures and a vain man's complaint, and by noon a stranger had found the question this whole circuit is going to be asking by the last meet. She found it, and she wrote it down, and then she very politely declined to answer it." She folded the ruling once. "The category is going to hold you exactly as long as nobody makes you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:217:"Yesterday morning at first light," said Karis, "you ran your entry in the loading yard behind the exchange, in the open, twice through, slowly and at speed. From the inn's back door I could see you. From the gate, so could a steward. Your quarterfinal opponent declined that exact entry twice that afternoon, and you said at the rope it was as if somebody had told him. This morning the Stone declined it too." She closed the notebook on her finger. "Somebody did tell them. It was sold inside a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:As a young man he had gone to the Bronze trials twice and come home both times, and after the second time he had stopped going and started lifting stone. He was a Stone Path, of the rooting kind. He fought as close to the other man as he could get, and then closer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:67:He read it the way he had been taught to read another officer's product, long ago, at a long table under an old man whose name nobody in the office used any more. The first time through, he read it for what it said. The second time, he read it for how it had been made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:It had come for a reckoning, and it had been given a quarterfinal, won in three, by a fighter who had made the result certain in the first exchange and then declined to make it hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:131:It was the travelling plate, the small twin of the one in the wing at home, a brass-faced disc on a short arm with its dial set into the housing. It had come upriver in a felt bag in the bottom of the case, under the seals. The guild had lent the panel its proving frame, an iron thing the millwrights used to test shafts, with a weight that ran down a rail on a pawl, and the guild's own millwright stood by to work it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:*Inventory, while I'm here. Wind: five of five on hard boards yesterday, which is all the day sells free on that floor. Four went to the yard-master and one to the academy Irons, and the Bronze got a step. The hip after; the left calf tight from the exchange I held. Pressure: flat at the plate again, the right shoulder paying. The cap is four blows a sitting, and today took one. Compression: held at a sleeve's width from the ganger's hand. Not on any record here. Ember: none spent; nobody here asked for it. Shadow: deployment none. Still.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:He made himself price it first, as he priced any advice before he gave it. At the beginning of the year an advisory of three sentences had cost him nothing, because it had only declined to misread a charter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:He had been in the Compact forty years, and he knew that kind of quiet. He had sent it down the stairs himself, once or twice, to offices whose questions he had not wished to hear a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:65:Then he put it on the table and went through it a second time, slowly, with one finger moving down the margin. He stopped the finger at a place on the page and left it there. He took a long time over the figures under his finger, longer than any sum on that sheet could need.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:211:The town stood on a tongue of land between the two rivers, built up to the water on both sides. Both rivers were iron-grey and running hard, with ice along their edges in plates and shelves, and the place where they met was a long rough seam of broken water that never froze, even in the deep cold, because the two currents never agreed with each other long enough to let it. Barges lay up at the quays three deep, roped together, their masts down. The hall where the meet was held stood at the very point of the tongue, with water on three sides of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:115:The second time he held his feet, and held them too long. The reserve came in low and wide and took him round the waist like a man lifting a sack of meal, and Cael went down onto the oak hard enough to hear it, with no plan whatever about what was meant to happen next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-21.md:55:A Mire fighter worked by making the floor feel deeper than it was, and Lira had declined to put her feet where he wanted them. She had spent one burst, Karis said, and never once crossed the place he kept pointing her at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-21.md:211:The captain felt it too. He drew a second breath and planted again and declared the rear pane a second time, cleanly, and it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:131:The second time, she hid the gather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He accepted the tea and declined the shawl, and thanked them both. Then he told them that the mark had managed three hundred years without a shawl and would very likely manage one more night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:49:*Subject's method is her signature. Nothing reaches the floor with her that has not been on paper first, and whoever has read her paper has read the bout before it begins. Pressed for time, she goes back to shapes she has already written down. She does not invent well, knows it, and declines to try.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:1:# Chapter 36 — Second Time
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:63:"Second time's the real rating," she said, loud enough for the rail. She said it the way a dealer says what an animal is worth after she has ridden it twice. "The first time anybody can be lucky. The second time is what you are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:79:*Brom fought carefully today,* Cael wrote that night. *He fought a woman who had watched him for a week and a man who had watched him for a season, both at once, from the same floor, and he gave the first a real bout and the second nothing at all. She said the second time is the real rating. I think she's right. The first look at anybody is a story somebody tells. The second is a measurement.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:161:"It was twice," said Cael. "The second time was the corner under the dais. You'd already spent the burst. She went the long way round and you were there anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:39:At the warm-up floor across the street, where the house had its first allotted hour, a knot of fighters from two delegations Cael did not know stopped talking as he came in, and did not start again until he had crossed the floor and gone out of the far door. At midday a Concourse steward at the gallery gate checked his credential against her printed list, and then checked it a second time. She was not doubtful of it. She plainly wanted to be able to say, afterward, that she had checked it herself. In the street a woman selling hot bread looked at him and then at a sheet pinned to the side of her barrow, and then at him again, and gave him a roll for nothing and would not take his copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:195:"I know. I heard them." Cael turned round again. The tail of the column was going away up the processional way, the blue and silver getting smaller against the lit tiers of the Concourse. "Everything in that line was put where it was for somebody standing where I'm standing. If I'd read her just now, I'd have read her the way they laid her out for me." He watched the last riders turn the corner toward the delegation quarter. "I'll take my first look at the procession, from wherever the marshals put me, on the tournament's own floor. Until then, all of it is Auremont's publishing. I decline to review the publishing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:It was a plain document, a plan of twelve seats drawn in a clerk's neat hand, with a name-plate entered against each. The six observation seats came first, then the district officers, then a liaison seat, then two left for officials of the host city. He read along them in order. One, two, three. Eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:231:There was no plate entered against it. Where the name should have been there was a short instruction in the same clerk's hand. *Seat twelve: held. No plate. Stewards to keep the seat clear for the length of the sitting.* Beneath that, in the column for the grade of the officer for whom a seat was held, the clerk had written one word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Then he did a thing he had done perhaps a dozen times in forty years. He noticed a thought beginning, a long one with a shape to it, and he declined to let it go on. It was not that the thought was improper. It was that it had nothing yet to stand on but an empty chair, and he had spent his working life refusing to let a thought carry more weight than the evidence under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:He carried it up the guesting-house stair the same way and set it on the long table beside his plate, upright, against the water jug, where it stayed all through supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:He followed her along the back of the gallery, past the public benches and behind the row's plated chairs, to the end, where the grey rail-cloth ran on in front of a chair with a pale square on its back and two empty screw holes. She lifted the cloth for him and let it fall again when he had sat down, and went back to her stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:25:He had read the seating plan in his room, a week before the opening. He had written one line in his day book about it and declined to think the long thought that came after the line. He declined it again now. A held chair was a courtesy. A steward who knew a man's grade by his face had been told it by somebody, and that was all that could be said about it with the evidence in hand, and he had spent forty years refusing to say more than the evidence would hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:43:What he saw in the second exchange he had never seen done on a floor. The boy declined to be told a story. Each of the showman's passages leaned toward its ending, and the bowl leaned with it, and at the moment before the ending the boy was quietly somewhere else, at no speed at all, until the passage had nothing left to land on. It was all in the open, inside the rule, in front of everyone, and as far as Vastin could tell not one person in the building was looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:75:He went along the back of the row without hurrying, behind the plated chairs, past Ilsev, who did not turn, and past Havel, who did. He nodded to Havel. He went down the east stair, where the young steward stood aside for him with her list against her chest, and out by the side door into the street, where the afternoon was going gold over the roofs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:263:"It's the second time, Cael. The same charter. The same people writing it. The same quiet, both times, in the exact place where anybody else would have put a qualifier." She turned to go in. "Once is a slip. Twice is starting to look like a door somebody built on purpose. A note for after Norhold."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:99:The second time, when the passage began to build, Cael stood still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:227:He was faster to the line without the show to carry. His entries came straight and short and early, from nowhere, with no sentence in front of them to warn of them, and the read still gave Cael his weight half a beat early, but now half a beat was all there was. Twice in the first half of the exchange the plain cut came close enough that Cael felt the air of it on his cheek. The second time, the hip told him what it thought of the step he had used to get away, and he lost a beat recovering.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:103:The Halcenvane rail went quiet all along its length, because they knew what they were watching, and nobody else in the hall did. The file had a line in it about this, the cruellest line on the third page, and Ivenne had written it. *Pressed for time, she goes back to shapes she has already written down. She does not invent well, knows it, and declines to try.* Ivenne was standing twenty feet away, waiting on that line. If that sentence was still true, she had already lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:195:"Mine is Zerin," she said. "I lost to her over four exchanges with the whole city watching, and I've been living on what that bought me ever since. Best thing that's happened to me since Fenmark put my things outside the door." She came away from the window and sat on the end of the bench. "And you've spent a year being measured by things that can't take your weight. Plates and panels and tables and men with rods. There's one person in this city whose measure of you would be worth having, and this morning she asked for it with one word." She looked at him. "I said somebody had to argue against. I never said it was me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:"I know. I've been thinking about the second half." He looked round at them. "Plan to have fewer. That's not plan to have none. You can't have none. Nobody can. Rooke knows that better than anybody." He stood up and began to clear his own plate, which he never did. "So the question isn't how many I've got. It's which ones he gets to keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:High on the east side, over the far rail, the Compact's row was full except for one chair. Ilsev sat in the first seat with her lap-desk on her knees. Havel was in the second. At the end of the row, behind the grey rail-cloth, the twelfth chair stood empty, with its brushed seat and its pale square where a plate should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:99:The second went in the half-beat after one of Brom's drives, when the weight he had thrown forward had to come back before he could throw it again. Cael had charted that gap on the oak at home through a whole year of sessions, and he had never once seen an opponent find it live. Marek found it on the second time of asking. The flag went up for Rhagen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:199:It was real. His whole doctrine said to take it. And he stood there and looked at what it had cost, and added that to everything else the morning had cost, and Cael saw him decide that it was not worth the price. It was the first time all tournament Cael had seen Marek decline anything he had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:101:"I can't drill that. It isn't a technique. It's a habit of looking." He shut the sheet. "So look. Every floor they build you between now and the end of this sitting, you walk it twice, and the second time you walk it you find me the line nobody's pricing. Write it down. Then tell somebody. A thing you've only written down is a thing you've found too late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:Brom was opposite him, with his wrapped forearms resting on the table and a plate of something in front of him that had certainly not been there when the proprietor opened her door. As Cael sat, a girl from the kitchen came and put down a basket of bread between them. Then she brought a pot of mussels in a dark, buttery broth, and a dish of potatoes cut thick and salted with grey salt, and went away again. Nobody had asked her for any of it. Brom looked at the mussels with mild interest, as a man looks at weather he had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:Lira read it aloud twice, the second time because Brom asked her to. Then she lifted her cup to the ledger-keeper of Ardenmere, and they all drank to her, and Karis said that Vell would certainly have entered the toast in the ledger as an item, dated and stamped, if she had been there to see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:157:The captain tried Cael twice that exchange. Cael did not realise it until the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:161:What came back the first time was Lira, at its edge, before it had properly arrived, sent by one word a breath earlier that Cael did not remember deciding to say. What came back the second time was Brom's bulk, a yard along the slope from where it had been, standing in the guard's way as if he had always meant to stand there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:149:She braided him down the east side, mast to mast. He paid for every step of it in coin he did not have. The sixth burst went, and the seventh, and the seventh was past six and his hip told him about it at once, a hot line from the crest to the inside of the knee; *the bill comes in the morning*, the minute said, and here it was, arriving early. He put his right palm to the barrier twice more and pushed, and the push threw him along the wall each time, and the right shoulder kept the count. Two. Three. Twice he got near enough to her to touch. She had come in close, the second time, to see what the left side would do. Both times she turned her forearm into his reach and took the contact on it. Both times he let the spark go anyway, into the forearm, small. It was past the two contacts the circle allowed in public. It was the minute's to spend. It bought him a step each time: she drew the arm back from the heat, and the lane she was laying drew back with it by a hand's breadth, and he went through the hand's breadth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:The second time was in the north bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-41.md:301:*Iron couldn't seed me. That was the first answer, on a roster at the start of the year, and I took it as a technicality. Silver couldn't find my edges this afternoon, and I took that, at the barrier, as a compliment. Neither of them was a technicality and neither was a compliment. They were the same answer given twice, the second time more slowly.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:25:The second time through, Karis danced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:At the end of the row was the twelfth chair. It had been held, unplated, for thirteen days before anybody sat in it, and had been empty again since the day of the duelist. There was a man in it now. He wore the plain grey of an inspecting officer and nothing on his collar. Seln said he had come in by the east stair an hour before the brackets began, alone, with no case and no clerk, and had sat down, and had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Then the protocols were read, and then they were read again. Every bout above Silver had them read twice, Seln had said, so that nobody could claim afterward not to have heard. The referee and the panel each held the power to stop the bout, either without the other, and nobody could appeal it. There were two healers at each fighters' gate. If fire went where nobody meant it to go, the masts would take it, and the cables would carry it down into the trench, and the trench would carry it off toward the east break, out of the bowl. On the second reading Cael listened for the one thing he had marked on the first. Every line of it was measured in tiers. Every threshold in it, for when a healer should stand and when a bout should stop, was set against a fighter's tier. There was one fighter in the bout to whom none of it could be set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:And as he read her he understood what she was doing. Her lanes were not full. He had seen her full lane on the trial floor and these were not that. They ran a little short, and a little shallow, and each one came at a slightly different speed from the one before, as if she were turning a dial. She was not trying to finish him. She was finding out where he stopped. It was what Gault did with a plate before he measured anything against it: take the baseline first, honestly, so that every reading after it would mean something. She was taking his. And then she would fight a little above it, so as not to waste either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:7:She read the entry first, once, as she read everything. She read it a second time for the words alone, as she had been taught to do, long ago, by a woman who read a form the way a jeweller looks at a stone. *Under*, not *by*. She noted that. Somebody had been careful, at the end of a long night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:15:The designation in the enrollee's file had been her question for a long while. She had first sent it up from Greyvane, plainly, asking who had entered the mark and on whose authority. Twice it had come back down to her with the same two sentences, *no originating authority of record*, and the second time a third sentence latched on behind them, saying the same nothing more firmly. So last year at Halcenvane, on the fifteenth of Reaping, she had sat at the long table in the emptied hall and filled in a second referral, fuller, every date and reference in its order, and marked it a level higher than the office that had answered. In all her years she had never before filed twice on one matter. A question nobody answered was a question to be asked again, through the next door up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:95:Then the delegation head stood up. He did it slowly, as if his knees had begun to object to such things. He said that his house wished to enter its objection on the record, and he entered it, in full, in words that were careful and grave and plainly felt: what a program owed the people in it, the lack of any precedent, the house's request that the office decline the filing. Umber heard him out without once looking away from him. When the man had finished he did not sit down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:165:At Auremont's place on the floor the pine case was unfastened a second time. Auremont's own people sleeved the banner to Auremont's staff, with the same four ties in the opposite order, and raised it. Then the house's procession took it round the floor once, close under the barrier, slowly, so that every person in the bowl could see it go by at the height of their eyes. Silver on grey. The mark's own flag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:169:"And what it costs." He did not look away. "Every plate anybody's ever set in front of me read me against those rates. Every shop's file was written against them. They're why nobody's ever quite known what to ask of me. After tomorrow whatever I show is in the book, and nobody will ever again measure me against less. There's no getting it back into the box." He put both palms down on the table. "A rate you have shown is a rate you have shown forever."
