editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:101:She wrote it while they watched, slowly, in the hand she kept for papers that strangers would read long afterwards, and she wrote it twice, a draft and then a fair copy, with nothing crossed out on the second. Then she turned the fair copy round and pushed it across the table to him, and he copied it into Hesk's book under the minute from two nights before, in his best hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:33:Cael knew what the two lines were before he read them. On the night of the mock bouts, at the trestle's end on the second training floor, he had watched her tear a strip from her scoring card and lay it in Rooke's old manual, and close the book on it, and say nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:"I know. I've been thinking about the second half." He looked round at them. "Plan to have fewer. That's not plan to have none. You can't have none. Nobody can. Rooke knows that better than anybody." He stood up and began to clear his own plate, which he never did. "So the question isn't how many I've got. It's which ones he gets to keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:High on the east side, over the far rail, the Compact's row was full except for one chair. Ilsev sat in the first seat with her lap-desk on her knees. Havel was in the second. At the end of the row, behind the grey rail-cloth, the twelfth chair stood empty, with its brushed seat and its pale square where a plate should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:237:Umber had seen it in the second exchange, and been sure of it in the third. Every gap the small one found, the big one had left open and set a price on, and the prices were exact, a fair charge for every one. Except one. One price on the board had been a fraction low all morning, the first gap, high on the right shoulder. A careless eye would read that as the big one's mistake. Umber did not have a careless eye. He had spent forty years training it out of himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:269:Lira found hers inside a minute. She read her pool from the top line down: an Ash Path fighter from a southern house in the first round, a middle seed, and a second round that would want looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:293:Ephram read his pool twice, and then once more with his finger on it, and Cael read it over his shoulder. The first round was the coast house's second entry, the house whose coach had nodded on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:227:The steward came over then with the format. He was a cheerful, thickset man who had plainly been warned, and had read the provision twice, and was proud of having it by heart. He also had news. The mill town's Iron entry was too large for a single draw, because the guild yards had entered their journeymen, so the host had split it at the fifth rank, as the charter allowed. Ranks one to four would fight their draw on the main floor. Ranks five and up would fight theirs in the second room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:7:She read the entry first, once, as she read everything. She read it a second time for the words alone, as she had been taught to do, long ago, by a woman who read a form the way a jeweller looks at a stone. *Under*, not *by*. She noted that. Somebody had been careful, at the end of a long night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:47:That night, in his narrow room at the inn behind the east tower, he wrote a fifth. At its head he put the line from the return, which he had read upside down on her desk in perhaps three seconds and found he had entire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:*The Archmarshal Vastin asks the enrollee for one hour, before the houses leave the city. Nothing will be written by either party. It would be better if nothing were brought to write with. The timekeepers' office in the east tower has lent a room for the purpose; it belongs to neither the adjudication office nor the Compact. He is free to say no; it will count against him nowhere, with no one, at any time; and that sentence is not a formality.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"The first four are words you use about a fault," said Vastin. "A thing gone wrong in the record, which a careful office would like to find and mend. You find a fault so that you can take it out." He moved his left hand an inch on the wood. "The second four are words you use about a thing you mean to keep in a box. I went through the Compact's charter for them, article by article, one evening. They aren't in it. You can't write *handling* about a question. You can only write it about an answer you've already got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:Every house on the continent kept a copy of Rhagen's book, and Halcenvane's had come north in Rooke's trunk; Ephram had borrowed it at the second waystation and read it through twice on the tailboard. He found the page he wanted without looking for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:She won the final in four exchanges, two touches to one, and she fought it off Ephram's page. She never once read the Stone's shoulders near the west rope; she read his feet, below the moving air, from the first exchange. The Stone took the first touch anyway, because he was good, with a heavy quick step off the warm flags that she saw and could not quite get clear of. Lira took the second exchange's touch from below, as Ephram had done. In the third the Stone began, slowly, to give ground toward the north windows, a step and then another, inviting her after him onto the wet slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:"Item. Heat-shadow. The air over the stove end moves, and if you read shoulders you read them late. He knew that, and I didn't. Cost: the second exchange." He turned a page. "Item. Floor speed. The north flags carry a skin of damp off the windows, and they're a hand faster than the rest of the floor. He walked me onto them twice. Cost: the second exchange and the fourth." He turned another. "Item. In the fourth I committed early because my foot travelled. That one's mine. I'd seen the flags by then and I still didn't look at them, because I was too busy looking at his feet. Cost: the bout."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:241:He fought the first exchange the careful way. The Blade came off his mark fast and early, as Rooke's sheet had said he would, and the read gave Cael his weight before it moved, as it always did. Cael gave ground and waited, and gave ground again, and took the touch late and clean when the Blade's second commitment left his ribs open on the turn. It was the kind of touch he had been taking all season, and the panel would mark it the way they had marked the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:95:But the captain's guard lay on every way over the hill like a wet blanket. It did not stop anybody. It cost them. Every stride uphill into it was a stride at half speed, and every second at half speed on the bank gave Rhagen's two holders in the north time to step across. Lira took the north-west platform twice and was taken off it twice, by a holder the slope had let arrive first. Ephram himself went over at the east end once, behind the stack, and found a Rhagen Shield waiting at the bottom with her hand already out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:167:High up in the west tower's box the trial's engagement scribe, whose whole work was to write each settled exchange of touches against the name of one fighter, sat for four seconds with her pen lifted over the sheet. Then, under the second exchange, she put three names on a single line: Karis, for the catch; Cael, for the reading; Brom, for the redirect.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:223:The standings were the oldest thing at the sitting, Bracken had said at supper, older than the brackets, and almost entirely made of paper. Every house that had fought formed up on the floor at the eighth bell in the order of its count, lowest first. A west-tower clerk on a box read each one out. A validation officer in Compact grey sat at a small table under a lamp with a second copy of the sheets, and initialled every line as he finished it, and he did not go on until she had. At the north end of the floor stood the Silver Standard, furled on its staff and bound with a strap, with one of the colour-guard at either side. It would not be unbound before the closing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:237:Later that night, at the adjudication office's reconciliation of the day's sheets, the senior clerk asked the engagement scribe to account for the line in the second exchange with three names on it. She did not try to account for it. She wrote in the margin of the sheet instead, in a small hand, and the note went into the office's record of the trial and was read aloud in the west tower more than once in the years after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:*The second finding is the one that matters. I have charted Cael at every rail since Greyvane. Today was the first day I understood what it costs him that nobody can study him back. I left my own file behind for an exchange and a half, and it emptied me to the bottom. He has no file to leave. Every time he walks onto a floor he walks out as somebody nobody has ever written down, and he does it alone, and in all this time he has not once said in my hearing that it was hard. I have written "unreadable" in my notes about him as though it were luck. It is labour. Every day, unpaid, and with nobody to check the work. I ought to have seen it sooner.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:139:Karis came out with salve on both palms and the left wrist strapped, and she fought him as she had fought Ivenne in the second exchange, plainly, on her feet, with no frame and no plan, making her contacts in passing where his pushes were going to be. For an exchange it worked very well. He had never met a caster who would not cast, and he spent the first exchange pushing at structures that were not there, like a man shouldering a door that has already been taken off its hinges. Karis took two points off him in passing. He took one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:11:Twenty-five was a strong Iron figure. Everybody on the floor who could read a board knew it, and so did she. It sat in the same band as half the Iron semifinalists of the cycle. And the semifinal before it had been twenty-four, against a fighter the gallery-sheets called buried, and a bout in the second round had gone five exchanges, and the line on the young man's certificate, the line the registry had written and the registry stood behind, said Copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:211:Karis stood on her mark afterward with her hands held out from her sides and her breath coming hard, and the second floor gave her the long low sound craft-readers give, which is not a cheer and is better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:303:Seln was sitting in the second wagon with the travel file open on his knees, as he had sat for ten days, copying something from one sheet to another in a hand too small to read from the tailboard. He did not look up at the hill or at Lira. Once, when the wagon jolted, he lifted the pen clear of the page and waited for the wheel to find the road again, and then went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:203:He had watched grammar change before, in his time. It was never announced. A word that had lived for years in one kind of paper would begin to turn up in another kind, a little out of place, like a farm cart in a city street. Nobody remarked on it the first time, or the second. By the tenth time it had stopped looking out of place, and the people who used it had forgotten that it had ever been anywhere else. He had once seen *resettlement* travel in that way from a report on a flooded valley to a report on a quarrelsome town, in less than a year, and nobody who used it in the second report had ever read the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:225:He wrote the first answer long, and then struck it through corner to corner and wrote it again in ink on a clean sheet. He did the same with the second and the third. It took him most of the evening, because each answer had to be built so that a reader could not lift a sentence out of it and use the sentence to mean the opposite.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:263:The next morning he came in at the second bell, as always. He read the day's first bundle, as always. And then, before he had opened the second, he wrote out a direction in his own hand and gave it to his clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:147:At the second wharf there was a hiring board, a plain black board with chalk on it, and a dozen men stood in front of it reading. *Grain lighter. Six hands. Copper will do. Iron preferred. Dinner found.* Under that, *Timber, the long dock, two days. Iron or better.* None of the men reading it had ever stood on a tournament floor, Cael thought, or wanted to. They knew exactly what a rank was. It was what a man could lift, and for how long, and what he could be paid for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:The betting rows had done something new. The office had let it be known the day before that it did not care for it. For the whole sitting the rows had kept their old frame on the wall, *NO BOOK ON THE DEMONSTRATION BOUTS*, and nobody had touched it. This morning there was a second board beside it. It did not offer odds on who would win. Everybody already knew who would win. It offered odds on the figure. The bands of the Standard were chalked down it in a column, from Iron at the bottom to Gold at the top, cut into half points, with a price against every one. Men were queueing to buy them. Cael read the board through once on his way past, from top to bottom, and saw that there was no line on it anywhere for no figure at all. It had not occurred to anybody to offer one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Then the protocols were read, and then they were read again. Every bout above Silver had them read twice, Seln had said, so that nobody could claim afterward not to have heard. The referee and the panel each held the power to stop the bout, either without the other, and nobody could appeal it. There were two healers at each fighters' gate. If fire went where nobody meant it to go, the masts would take it, and the cables would carry it down into the trench, and the trench would carry it off toward the east break, out of the bowl. On the second reading Cael listened for the one thing he had marked on the first. Every line of it was measured in tiers. Every threshold in it, for when a healer should stand and when a bout should stop, was set against a fighter's tier. There was one fighter in the bout to whom none of it could be set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:37:The read showed him the weight long before the walk arrived. It showed the ganger's weight settle onto his left foot for the second step, and Cael was not there for the second step. He had gone right, a short quick step, out of the drover's line and along it, so that the big man's arms closed on air at the place where he had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:55:Every one of those wide slow walks had been built from the ground up, with the feet set and the arms spread and the weight committed to the second step. Every one had been thrown away at the second step, and built again from nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:245:The standings were read from the guild hall steps at dusk on the Second-day, with the delegations standing in the square below in their colours and the lamps being lit along the porch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:249:There had been a bout or two worth the trip. Lira had gone through the Iron draw without losing a touch she had not meant to give, and had won its final against the river academy's second entry in the last light of the afternoon, on a floor already half in shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:257:"Second of six. Lira: I'll see what four bursts in the opening bout cost you at the first dawn on the road, and you won't dress it up for me. Brom: I looked for a note to give you and didn't find one. Karis: you spent your second bout reading the judges' table, and it cost you an exchange you didn't need. I'll lend you that exchange this time. I'm telling you so you know it's a loan. Ephram: the Blade beat you on figures, not on the floor, and you know the difference, so I needn't tell you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:53:The soup at the waystation was the worst soup of the season, which was saying a good deal after the slate country. It was grey, and it tasted mostly of the pot, and Brom was eating his second bowl of it with the steady resignation of a man stoking a stove. Ephram had given up and was eating bread. Gault had his case on the bench beside him, one hand resting on its lid. The reserves were arguing quietly about a bout neither of them had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:191:On the Fifth-day Cael took his coursework to the wing's counter, as he did every Fifth-day, and stood in the line behind a Mire second-year and two Current boys. Seln took the folder, read the cover sheet from the name to the signature at his even pace, stamped it, and tore off the slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:207:The wagons left on the Second-day with a hired carriage behind them for Withrow and Bracken. The carriage was black and narrow and had been, the wagoner said, a magistrate's once. Withrow sat in it with a rug over her knees and a book open and did not read it. Bracken sat opposite with a box of papers on his knees and read every one of them twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:235:She stood in front of the board with her hands in her pockets and read down her half of it slowly: the Mire in the first round, then whoever came through the second pairing, and in the semifinal, if the seeds held, the third seed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:177:For two days she had watched the frame in the north tunnel, with her hood up, from the back of whatever crowd was there. On the first morning she had stood through the steward's whole hour and seen him unlock his case and read his book and lock it again. That afternoon she had gone back, she could not have said why, and been there when the Silver from the east house stood in front of the empty line and put his form back inside his coat. On the second morning she had been in the warehouse, taking a committee apart, while two hundred people stood in the rain to watch nothing happen; the coopers' boys had told her about it at noon. She had read the broadsides asking who would fight the enrollee. She had heard the betting rows cry their price on nobody. She had given the city two days to find him somebody from below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:197:The program ate together in the mornings, at a long table in the front room of its quarters, under a window that did open, because nobody had thought to change it. Zerin was there already, with a cup and a sheet of times. The lattice-breaker was there, eating porridge with great method. Two of the design staff were arguing quietly about a margin at the far end. Nobody looked up when she came in, because nobody ever did; she was always the first or the second down, and always on time, and always the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:133:He could see her doing it, which was the strange part. She had been working at it since the mill town, since Karis spent her left-hand tell in front of a crowd and she promised to fix it. All through the cold term she had drilled on the far end of the oak, alone, folding the gather for each burst down into the landing of the one before. She did it now, and did it well. She came out of her first burst already set for the second, and there was no moment of settling for the read to find, only a small hard landing and then she was gone again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:Bracken collected the notice himself from the trial office at the second bell, and brought it up the guesting-house stair to the long table, where they were all eating breakfast, and laid it down beside the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:39:At the warm-up floor across the street, where the house had its first allotted hour, a knot of fighters from two delegations Cael did not know stopped talking as he came in, and did not start again until he had crossed the floor and gone out of the far door. At midday a Concourse steward at the gallery gate checked his credential against her printed list, and then checked it a second time. She was not doubtful of it. She plainly wanted to be able to say, afterward, that she had checked it herself. In the street a woman selling hot bread looked at him and then at a sheet pinned to the side of her barrow, and then at him again, and gave him a roll for nothing and would not take his copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:223:There were the liaison papers between the observation office and the tournament's adjudication office, courteous and dull. There was the rotation of the observation officers through the three weeks, with their seats and their hours, and the names on it were names he knew. He found Ilsev in the first seat, as he had expected, and Havel in the second. There were the instructions that had gone out under each paper, and the acknowledgments that had come back. He read them all and found nothing in them that a careful office would not have written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:The squad had come up to the second training floor at the seventh bell of the Sixth-day: a long upper room over the Blade hall, its boards laid on joists that gave a little underfoot like the yard's oak, with tall windows down one side and a row of lamps already lit along the other. The five of them, the two third-year reserves, and Rooke. A trestle table stood by the door with stacks of paper on it under a stone. A board leaned against the end wall with the training calendar pinned to it, five weeks of it, every day ruled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:63:He came off the mark at half speed, then at a quarter, then at a walk. At a walk Cael could see it, and once he saw it he could not believe he had missed it twice. Ephram's front foot went forward and took the weight, honest weight, the real thing, enough of it that any read in the world would have reported it as an attack. But the back foot stayed. It stayed planted for the length of one heartbeat after the front had landed, holding a second share of the weight in reserve. Only when the man in front of him had answered the first share, had slipped and turned for home, did the back foot come off the floor and drive. The lath went in behind the evasion, into the space the evader had just vacated and was now coming back to fill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:73:The read had never lied to him. He had written it a dozen times, and it was true: weight and direction, early, never what a man meant by them. And here was a man who meant something by the order of his weights, and the read had told him the truth about the first and nothing about the second, because the second had not happened yet. It had reported honestly and he had answered honestly, and he had been hit, twice, by a thing that had been designed by somebody long dead in a house he had never seen to hit that kind of honesty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:111:They went again, and again, until the lamps were brighter than the windows. Cael beat the second weight four times in the next ten and ate the plain entry twice when he waited for a heel that never waited. At the rail Rooke wrote one line on his board, and Cael, passing it on the way to the water jug, read it upside down out of habit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:127:Cael read the stack fast and then a second time slowly, and the room around him went quiet, as rooms did when he did that. The read was no use on paper; paper had no weight. But he had a second thing that worked on paper, which was the habit of years spent reading men who wanted to be misread, and it found him more in Rooke's three-word margins than in the long lines of record beside them. A man who wrote *studies you* about an opponent was paying her the highest compliment Rooke owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:Fenmark: the house that had Kindled her at fourteen and read her Copper and sent her out into the world with six letters on a certificate. It had a fighter in the region's Iron bracket this year, a second-year, and Rooke had given him four words: *Good. Schooled. Expects respect.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:241:"*Seeded* is a word from the bracket rules. It's what the host does to fill a draw: it puts a fighter on a line according to his tier and rank. *Fielded* is a word from the sixth part. It's what a house does when it sends its people. The charter has both words. It never once lets them into the same paragraph." She drew a line across the memorandum with her fingernail, between the second sentence and the third. "This joins them so that one seems to follow from the other. So that a house could only send what a host could seed. The charter never says so. Not anywhere. I'd know. I've read it five times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:"Everything I put under it made it weaker." She was writing the second page and did not look up. "Anyone who reads that line and doesn't understand it won't understand it any better with me standing next to it explaining."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:129:"Now look at the sequence. It's the only part that matters." She lifted one finger from the stack. "First there was a house. I spent a good many years making it a house that does difficult things in writing, in front of witnesses, and gets them right." A second finger. "Then there were rules: their charter, written by their own board forty years ago, before you were born. Nobody here wrote a word of it." A third. "Then there were people who keep the rules as written. A registrar who signs true records because he can't bear to sign any other kind. A girl in a reading room who reads the whole of a thing, including the schedule between the fee table and the surgeons." She did not glance at Bracken by the door. "And last of all, there was you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:The floor, at least, did not wait for anybody. On the Fourth-day Ephram came to the second session with his shoulders held so still that he looked like a man carrying a full tray across a crowded room. He threw the second-year entry at Cael twenty times in an hour, and Cael beat the second weight twelve of them by the heel alone, because there was no longer any shoulder to read. On the ninth throw he ate the plain entry clean across the ribs and let it pass. On the fourteenth Ephram's shoulders dropped again from tiredness, and Cael called it aloud before the heel had moved, and Ephram muttered something at his own shoulders and went back to his chalk. Rooke's board, at the end, said only *Better. Both.* Cael read it upside down on his way to the water jug, and carried it back up to the residence with him like a coin in his pocket, and turned it over at supper when the sum came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:205:Cael took it from his pigeonhole in the cloister and read it standing there, under the lamp by the porter's lodge, with second-years going past him in both directions on their way to supper. He had stopped trying to wait until the stair with Hesk's letters. He had never once managed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:261:"Since the second day of drafting. You went quiet at supper that night, when Karis came in with her hair down." She stood and brushed off her coat. "Come and eat your own bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:Zerin arrived where she had meant to be, a tenth early as always, and Lira was already standing there. Every piece of Zerin had been built to arrive first. For half a beat she had arrived second, and her body did not know what to do about it. Her weight hung.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:173:She stood on her chalk in the light from the glass with her hands at her sides, and her eyes went round the floor, corner to corner, rope to rope. Cael knew exactly what she was doing. She was reading the second exchange back to herself, the way he read the notebook. It was the first time all tournament that he had seen anybody's study run in her direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:113:He read the second. The subject had trained per the house's schedule. The schedule was pinned to a board in the long upper room in the coach's capitals, and Seln had read it on his way to somewhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:He read the third. The capabilities exercised were consistent with those on record. The subject had spent two ignitions a round against the Shield reserve on the oak, every second day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:145:The roster was on the board in the morning, and by the first bell the whole house had read it. By the second bell the provisional seedings had gone up beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:391:She looked at him. "The moment was three years of work, and I already had it. Vell's floor. Greyvane. The crown. That counting room at the station, and the frame, and the four seconds. Every one of those was the moment. I just kept waiting for them to be in the room for it. And somewhere on a wagon I stopped needing them in the room at all, and I never noticed I'd stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:469:On the season's count the house was still second, by less than before, with one meet left on the card and the threshold close enough to see. Rooke read the board from the bottom up, folded his copy into his coat, and said, "Wagons."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-21.md:35:His first challenger was the confluence-house Blade who had written *the town asked me*, a quick, merry young man of the house's second rank, and every time he moved the whole barn roared for him as though he were a brother. Cael took a touch in the first exchange the careful way, late, on the turn. In the second the Blade came off his mark with the barn on his back, a wall of sound rolling down off the vault to the ropes, and Cael ran item two for the first time: *a conceded point when the light is behind me, the floor soft, or the crowd loud.* He let the noise take a little off his read, a little more than it needed to, and was a quarter-beat slow to turn from the second feint, and the Blade's palm found his shoulder. "Touch. Confluence." The barn went up like a bonfire. It was a true point; a loud room does cost a fighter something. Cael took the second touch in the fourth exchange from firm planks, with one burst, *one of five*, and the steward called it, "Two to one. Bout to Halcenvane." The Blade grinned at him at the handshake with a story that would last him a lifetime in a town this size. "Wait till you see them for the bargeman," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:The broadside on the breakfast table had it too. It had the bout in two columns and a woodcut of the captain on the oak with his guard drawn round him as a great bubble of fine lines, and Cael, very small, running up the hill at him. That was the story everybody had paid for, and the paper gave them their money's worth. But down at the foot of the second column, after the figure and the cheering, the writer had put one more paragraph, and it was the one the whole table read twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:227:He was faster to the line without the show to carry. His entries came straight and short and early, from nowhere, with no sentence in front of them to warn of them, and the read still gave Cael his weight half a beat early, but now half a beat was all there was. Twice in the first half of the exchange the plain cut came close enough that Cael felt the air of it on his cheek. The second time, the hip told him what it thought of the step he had used to get away, and he lost a beat recovering.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:229:The bowl, which had been a little restless through the second exchange, went quiet. The craft-readers in the lower tiers leaned forward. This was a bout now, and they knew it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:17:The second page was the clause's history, and the third was as good an argument as he had read from a provincial house in some years. It cited a schedule he had not known existed; he went to his own shelf and turned to it, and it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:149:She braided him down the east side, mast to mast. He paid for every step of it in coin he did not have. The sixth burst went, and the seventh, and the seventh was past six and his hip told him about it at once, a hot line from the crest to the inside of the knee; *the bill comes in the morning*, the minute said, and here it was, arriving early. He put his right palm to the barrier twice more and pushed, and the push threw him along the wall each time, and the right shoulder kept the count. Two. Three. Twice he got near enough to her to touch. She had come in close, the second time, to see what the left side would do. Both times she turned her forearm into his reach and took the contact on it. Both times he let the spark go anyway, into the forearm, small. It was past the two contacts the circle allowed in public. It was the minute's to spend. It bought him a step each time: she drew the arm back from the heat, and the lane she was laying drew back with it by a hand's breadth, and he went through the hand's breadth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:He was still reading, and the creases were still clear in his head, every one of them, coming to him half a beat before their lanes as they had since the second bell, however the rest of him was faring. It was the one thing in the whole ring that had not cost him anything yet. But the body had to carry what the read told it, and the body was slower every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:Lira read it aloud twice, the second time because Brom asked her to. Then she lifted her cup to the ledger-keeper of Ardenmere, and they all drank to her, and Karis said that Vell would certainly have entered the toast in the ledger as an item, dated and stamped, if she had been there to see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:231:Their second night on the road was at a coaching inn where the hills gave out onto the plain. The wagons stood in the yard under the frost, and the inn had only two rooms and a landing to spare, so the delegation slept where it could. At the inn's counter, while they were taking their keys, the courier who carried the region's meet records came in out of the dark and dropped his bundle on the board. A man in a travelling coat who had been sitting by the fire got up, and bought a copy of each meet's record from the bundle, and paid without asking the price, and went back to his chair and began to read. Cael saw the man's finger stop, about a third of the way down the wool town's leaf, at the place where the exhibitions were. Then the man turned the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:39:Gault had the second wagon's front bench, with the case on his lap and both hands resting on its lid, and whenever a wheel found a rut he steadied it without looking down. Karis read. The two reserves played a game with pebbles on a board chalked onto the wagon bed, and argued about its rules, which neither of them seemed to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:57:Rooke stopped in front of the second board. He read it from top to bottom without expression, and then turned his back on it and never looked at it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:285:Her second burst did not test anything. Each time the Stone shifted his weight from one planted foot to the other, there was a moment, half a beat long, when neither foot was quite reading. It was the gap a man leaves when he changes his grip on a heavy thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:201:The crowd read that one aloud, and it read it in four tones, one after another, as people do when a line on a board will not quite sit still. A second-year near the front said it slowly, like a word in a language he was learning. A Stone girl said it with a laugh in it, not unkind, as if the board had made a joke she had nearly understood. Somebody's older brother said it with relish, like good news about a horse. And a quiet voice at the back said it carefully, a thing that might break if he dropped it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:39:The first was Ephram, who knew what Cael had learned in ten days, and had spent those ten days learning what Cael read. He came straight off the mark with the second-year entry, front foot loaded, honest weight, the lath low; and Cael let the first share go by, and watched the heel, and the heel was flat, and flat, and then up, and he was already gone outside it. The lath passed a hand's breadth from his hip. Ephram did not seem surprised. He turned on the miss and came again, and this time the shoulders dropped with the front foot, everything at once, the plain entry, and Cael had to burst to get out from under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:He had six free on the sprung oak. This floor was not the oak, it was the second floor's boards, a little stiffer, a little less forgiving, and he knew from a month of mornings that six here was really five. The record said he had a burst that carried twenty feet and a read that looked like peculiar timing. The record had nothing in it about a burst shut off halfway through its paying, or about a contact that took a blow and did not give it back, or about a plate. So tonight, in front of a panel of five, he would fight with what the record held, at the rates the record held it, and not one thing more. It was the doctrine, and he had chosen it, and he had never before had to keep to it in front of people holding pencils.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:49:Cael read him: weight forward, left, the shoulders not dropped, so the front foot only, so wait. He waited, and the heel came up, and he went right and outside, and as he went he put his palm on Ephram's ribs, a touch, clean, on the line the second weight had just left open, and stepped off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:The second exchange was long, and it was Ephram's, because he had learned from the first. He stopped throwing the second-year entry at all and began to walk, as he had walked on the morning of the first supervised session, at every angle and every pace, the lath low, never committing. The read told Cael everything about where Ephram's weight was going and nothing about when he would choose to send it. Twice Cael read a step as an entry and moved, and twice it was only a step, and he had moved for nothing, and had spent a little hip on each. *Two. Three.* On the third burst he landed close to the rope. Ephram, who had been walking him there for half a minute without either of them saying so, came in with all of it at once and laid the lath along his forearm as the landing beat held him still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:63:The third exchange he spent learning Ephram's walk. He did not move for any step that did not drop the shoulders, and he let three of Ephram's feints go by so close that he felt the air of the lath. *Four* was a burst that took him clear of the rope and into the open middle of the ring, where Ephram could not herd him. Then Ephram, who had worked out that Cael was now waiting for shoulders, threw a second-year entry with his shoulders held perfectly still. It was the thing he had been practising for ten days in front of the long window's glass. Cael read the front foot, and waited for a shoulder drop that never came, and nearly ate the second weight entirely. He got out of it by a hand. He did not get a touch back. The exchange ran out on Rooke's glass with nobody scored.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:67:In the fourth Cael stopped trying to read Ephram's choices at all. He read only his weight, the old way, the first way, early, and answered the first share of everything. Ephram, who had spent the bout teaching him to wait, could not believe he had stopped waiting, and threw the second-year entry three times running into a boy who was simply no longer where the second weight was meant to find him. On the third of them Cael went inside instead of out, under the lath, and laid his palm on Ephram's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:437:Karis had read the order in the first exchange, and in the second she had begun to arrive at each mark half a beat before he finished it. Three and nought; two and one; three and nought.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:87:She came to everything. She had stood at the front of the draw with her hands folded, and at the trial's posting, and at the boards on every night of the brackets, and been seen. Tonight the place at the front where she would have stood had two dockmen in it. Cael looked twice, and on the second look he understood it as he understood a move on a floor. She was the most careful person he knew. She had not forgotten. Somewhere earlier, by herself, she had read this board, and decided it was not hers to stand at. She had left it to the five of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:151:"Half a second, and she wasn't even looking at you. She was on her way somewhere and you were in the road." He turned his back to the door and leaned on it. "In that ring she'll be looking at you from the moment you walk out. Nothing else. Every bit of her, on you." He thought about it, slowly. "I'd want that one, if I were you. Attention you can read. Traffic you can't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:189:In the second exchange Lira did not burst at all for the first half of the glass. She walked. She came at Karis at a walk and a run and a walk again, as Ephram did, never committing, and her left hand stayed shut. Karis waited for the hand, and it did not open. And then Lira went, from a walk, with her fingers still closed, and the burst took her past Karis on the left before Karis's eyes had found anything to read. She came back on Karis from behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:249:So Cael went through the arch into the second room, where the upper Iron had fought its draw all day, and found the rope there already crowded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:281:The second exchange belonged to the Blade, and it was a lesson in how good he was. He had seen the late turn once, and he would not be caught by it twice. He stopped stepping outside at all. He stood his ground and let the entry come, and when the front foot made its late half-turn he was already turning with it, and his own lath came up under Ephram's arm and touched his flank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:291:In the fourth exchange Ephram threw the second-year entry three times, and each time he threw it differently. The first time he did not turn the front foot at all, and the Blade, who was waiting to turn with it, turned with nothing and had to recover. The second time he turned it early instead of late, before the heel. The third time he threw the plain entry, everything at once, no second weight, the one he had mixed in on the very first morning on the second floor at home to keep Cael honest. And the Blade, who had spent three exchanges and a whole bout at the wool town learning to read a two-weight entry with great patience, read the one-weight entry as a two-weight entry for a quarter of a beat too long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:He heard every one, and the read, which he had carried onto every floor since Brom first stood across from him, did not fail him once. Every fold came to him half a beat before its lane, as it had all through the second exchange and the third, clean and early and true. And every one of them told him the same thing, which was where he could no longer stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:67:He read it the way he had been taught to read another officer's product, long ago, at a long table under an old man whose name nobody in the office used any more. The first time through, he read it for what it said. The second time, he read it for how it had been made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:241:On the second morning, when the company came across the yard to the exchange, it was not blank. Across the top of it, in a handicapper's quick chalk, somebody had written a single line, and a little crowd had gathered in front of it to read it and laugh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-22.md:175:It was not a family argument this time, but an exam between two people who had sat next to each other through the whole course and copied nothing from each other's slates. Karis knew all of Ephram's entries, and Ephram knew Karis read panels, and both of them knew the other knew, so that the whole bout had a faint air of two clerks auditing each other's books in public, politely, with nothing whatever to hide. Ephram took the first touch off the plain entry, the one he had been mixing in since the first squad session. Karis took the second exchange's touch on contact with the ignition, a single point of heat at the heel of her palm on Ephram's forearm as he turned. The steward hesitated, as stewards always did with Ember, and then called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-22.md:269:Lira was already moving. She had committed on purpose, a half-step, to give him the plain entry, because she had worked out in the second exchange that it was the only thing he had left that she had not seen twice. As his palm went out she turned inside it and touched his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:9:Lira's second round had gone by on the morning of the fourth day, before the city walk, and nobody had needed to say much about it afterward. This was the quarterfinal, the first bout on the morning's card, and the hall under the high glass was already half full when the two fighters came out to their chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:61:He did nothing else for the whole exchange, and nothing else was needed. Auremont's pair on the south platform were in the wrong country. Sooner or later the north would want them back, and short of the barrier's far ends there were exactly two holes in it, and one of them now had Brom in it like a cork in a bottle. The Silver tried it first. He came at the gap fast from the south side, saw what was standing in it, and stopped and went back, because there is no going through a gap that is already full. A little later he tried it again, and Brom's heavy hand found his shoulder in the gap's mouth, and the flag went up, and the Silver had to walk all the way to Auremont's gate to touch the post. After that nobody in blue used the second gap at all. They went round by the south-east stack instead, nearly the whole length of the floor, and the platform marshals counted every minute they spent on the way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:157:There were his two notes from the duelist's bout, in his own small hand. The first was about the second exchange, about a boy refusing another man's time, openly, at some cost to himself. The second began *Close.* And then: *Everything I saw today, I already had a word for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:161:"It was twice," said Cael. "The second time was the corner under the dais. You'd already spent the burst. She went the long way round and you were there anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:125:In the second he had read it. He took a point off a feint Ephram had not seen, and gave one back, and the bell went at one each. Three to two after two, Ephram's way, and the coast house's bench had begun to talk among themselves in low voices.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:119:He did it the same way for everybody. He took the form. He read it, every line, at an even pace, from the name to the signature. He stamped the received line, tore off the copy-slip along its rule, and gave the slip back. The Stone first-year got what the Mire third-year got, and the Mire third-year what the first-year got, down to the second it took to read the reason for the hours. Cael had worked out in his second month on the bluff what that was for. A man who read every form from every hand was a man nobody remembered reading anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:135:He had already turned back toward the copying table. By the time Cael had folded the slip into his coat the pen was in Seln's hand again and his head was down over a ledger, and a Blade second-year with ink on his cuff had stepped up behind and was laying his own form on the counter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:"Your next evaluation. The provision's second." Gault tipped his head at the calendar behind him, where a line in his own hand marked the half-month and read *Panel of record: Gault, with two. Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* "It falls inside the qualifying season. Under the old reading of the provision you'd have had to come home for it, wherever the season had taken you, and sit it here, in this wing, on this frame. The chancellor didn't care for that. She wrote to the registry's district office. The district office, to the astonishment of everybody who has ever written to a district office, agreed by return without a hearing." He unlaced his fingers to count on them. "The enrollment basis goes where the enrollee goes. The evaluation goes where the basis goes. So the evaluation goes where you go. If the delegation is in a mill town on the day, then the panel sits in the mill town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:161:"I read the rider." Karis looked at the candle. "There's a clause in it for when the healers go in, and another for when they carry someone out. A stoppage under that woman isn't a line in a book. She crossed his ground on the trial floor without even looking at him and he had half a second. Give her all her attention and keep him inside his rates, and there's nothing cheap about losing early. There may not be any getting up from it." She picked up the pen again. "That's the case argued. I've left it. Open it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:319:He walked back to where Seln stood, and looked west, with his arms hanging, as he looked at a rack of plates. The track ran back across the upland, pale, as far as the roofs of the station, with the water beyond, and the bare rise. There was no rider on it. There was no lantern, and no tin cup, and no man with a pencil on a top tier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:201:"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. The refusal was the Archmarshal's; the form's old column had only Gault's title to put it under. Gault sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:51:Cael came out onto the gallery with the satchel on his shoulder and looked down. The yard was flagged, with a pump in one corner and a stack of empty woolsacks under a pentice roof. In the middle stood the press, a thing as old as the building: two oak posts as thick as a man, a beam across their tops, and a great wooden screw coming down through the beam to a platen of black timber six feet square. A capstan with four long bars turned the screw. In sale weeks, the woman of the house had said, it took four men to bale a sack with it. It was not a sale week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:53:Brom stood under the platen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been turned over where it stood so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:289:Rooke looked at Lira, and she looked back, and neither of them said anything at all, since it had been said at dawn, over a book. Then he looked at Brom, who was at the tailboard with the last of the rope in his hands. Brom had stood in a district's frame two days ago and felt a plate come up warm, and had left the paper for it under a film of dust; and Rooke had heard everything on the hill by the second bell of any morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:225:*The seat notes the respondent's letter of the eleventh sitting, by which he declined further attendance, and enters the letter upon the record. The respondent not having appeared within the time the code allows, the petition is granted by default.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:85:*Four streets back from the seat, on the wharf side. The tall house with the brass plate. The third floor. The second bell after noon. Neither office's. Nothing written.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:*Subject attended to his course of study. Subject attended the seat's fourth sitting as directed. An instrument presented at the house's gate on the Sixth-day was declined by the house; subject was not seen at the refusal. Subject's floor work within the band of the year. Nothing further to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:155:Brom did not say anything to that. He stood in the market street with his forearms marked and his face quite calm, and Cael saw that it had been enough. Whatever a clerk wrote on whatever paper, a plate had come up warm under his boots and said what was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:138:"Offered to the proceedings at Ostrand under the code's two provisions; declined without citation as outside their schedule. The schedule itself is not disputed. One thing only is set down. A schedule that leaves no room for the one personal assessment of the subject the Compact holds was drawn for a purpose the evaluator was never told and cannot name from his desk. Where the outcome of an evaluation is settled before the evaluator arrives, it is not the subject who is being measured."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:The letter said where the rubbing had come from, in its first paragraph, with the instrument quoted whole: it was the seal off the escort order that the leader of four riders had read aloud in the house's outer court on the Sixth-day morning, and that the house had declined at its own gate and let ride away again down the bluff road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:The short frame was a simple machine: two timber uprights, a crossbar of iron that ran in greased slots between them, and under each end of the bar a spring as thick as a man's wrist. You stood beneath the bar and took it on your shoulders, or on your forearms crossed above your head, and somebody let the catches go, and the springs drove it down. A sigil frame at a district seat was a finer thing, with a plate that read what the practitioner's Path did under the weight. This one read nothing. It only pressed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:137:Lira took the slice back off his plate without asking and began to build the stack again herself. She laid the bottom slice down. She set the second on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:285:"Past the Line, then." Ephram stood with his hand on the file. "Then nobody will ever write a figure for you again. Not Gault, nor a panel, nor me." He was quiet for the space of two breaths. "I thought it might be that. I've thought so since the boxes went onto Bracken's shelf; I've eyes, and I walk the records hall on Third-days. I didn't know it was all of you." He looked at Brom, who had come in behind him, and Brom looked back, and whatever passed between the two of them had been settled in a harness room over a rack of iron plates and did not need a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:They checked the lines against station books, and house ledgers, and bout sheets, and each one stood. A subject had attended. A figure was as the house had entered it. An instrument had been declined at a gate, and the subject had not been seen at the refusal. Associations were unchanged. *Nothing further to report.* It was as clean a body of product as any reader there had handled, and one of them said so, and it was put in a minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:87:It knew what he had sent. It did not know what he had kept. There was no return that listed the things a quiet man had declined to write down. Nor was there any record of a records room on a top floor, in a district he had left twelve years before, where he had gone to look for a subject's travel papers. Nobody thought to ask for one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:*The Chancellor declined the order pending verification, on four grounds, as follows.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.

